Black holes are classified by the theory of their formation and by their mass (expressed in terms of {{solar mass}}, the mass of the Sun), but these criteria are intertwined. [[Stellar black hole]]s are formed by stellar collapse. The minimum mass of a black hole formed by stellar gravitational collapse is governed by the maximum mass of a neutron star and is believed to be {{solar mass|2-4}}.<ref>{{multiref2 |{{Cite journal |last1=Drischler |first1=Christian |last2=Han |first2=Sophia |last3=Lattimer |first3=James M. |last4=Prakash |first4=Madappa |last5=Reddy |first5=Sanjay |last6=Zhao |first6=Tianqi |title=Limiting Masses and Radii of Neutron Stars and Their Implications |journal=Physical Review C |date=2021 |volume=103 |issue=4 |article-number=045808 |doi=10.1103/PhysRevC.103.045808 |arxiv=2009.06441 |bibcode=2021PhRvC.103d5808D |display-authors=2}}|{{Cite journal |last1=Farr |first1=Will M. |last2=Sravan |first2=Niharika |last3=Cantrell |first3=Andrew |last4=Kreidberg |first4=Laura |last5=Bailyn |first5=Charles D. |last6=Mandel |first6=Ilya |last7=Kalogera |first7=Vicky |title=The Mass Distribution of Stellar-Mass Black Holes |journal=The Astrophysical Journal |date=2011 |volume=741 |issue=2 |page=103 |doi=10.1088/0004-637X/741/2/103 |arxiv=1011.1459 |bibcode=2011ApJ...741..103F |display-authors=2}}}}</ref> Hypothetical [[primordial black hole]]s, believed to have formed soon after the [[Big Bang]], could be far smaller, with masses as little as {{val|e=-5|u=grams}} at formation.<ref name="Carr">{{Cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=Carr |first1=Bernard |last2=Kohri |first2=Kazunori |last3=Sendouda |first3=Yuuiti |last4=Yokoyama |first4=Jun'Ichi |title=Constraints on Primordial Black Holes |journal=Reports on Progress in Physics |date=2021 |volume=84 |issue=11 |doi=10.1088/1361-6633/ac1e31 |pmid=34874316 |arxiv=2002.12778 |bibcode=2021RPPh...84k6902C }}</ref> These very small black holes are sometimes called [[micro black hole]]s.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Nakama |first1=Tomohiro |last2=Yokoyama |first2=Jun'Ichi |title=Micro Black Holes Formed in the Early Universe and Their Cosmological Implications |journal=Physical Review D |date=2019 |volume=99 |issue=6 |article-number=061303 |doi=10.1103/PhysRevD.99.061303 |arxiv=1811.05049 |bibcode=2019PhRvD..99f1303N }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Scardigli |first1=Fabio |title=Gravity Coupling from Micro-Black Holes |journal=Nuclear Physics B: Proceedings Supplements |date=2000 |volume=88 |issue=1–3 |pages=291–294 |doi=10.1016/S0920-5632(00)00788-X |arxiv=hep-th/9907150 |bibcode=2000NuPhS..88..291S }}</ref>

Stellar black holes can have a wide range of masses. Estimates of their maximum mass at formation vary, but generally range from {{solar mass|10-100}}, with higher estimates for black holes progenated by [[metallicity|low-metallicity]] stars.<ref name=Vink-2021>{{Cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=Vink |first1=Jorick S. |last2=Higgins |first2=Erin R. |last3=Sander |first3=Andreas A C. |last4=Sabhahit |first4=Gautham N. |title=Maximum Black Hole Mass Across Cosmic Time |journal=Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society |date=2021 |volume=504 |pages=146–154 |doi=10.1093/mnras/stab842 |doi-access=free |arxiv=2010.11730 }}</ref> Stellar black holes can gain mass via accretion of nearby matter, often from a companion object such as a star<ref name="lph97">{{Cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=Dunn |first1=R. J. H. |last2=Fender |first2=R. P. |last3=Körding |first3=E. G. |last4=Belloni |first4=T. |last5=Cabanac |first5=C. |title=A Global Spectral Study of Black Hole X-Ray Binaries |journal=Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society |date=2010 |volume=403 |issue=1 |pages=61–82 |doi=10.1111/j.1365-2966.2010.16114.x |arxiv=0912.0142 |bibcode=2010MNRAS.403...61D |doi-access=free }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Shao |first1=Yong |last2=Li |first2=Xiang-Dong |title=Population Synthesis of Black Hole X-Ray Binaries |journal=The Astrophysical Journal |date=2020 |volume=898 |issue=2 |page=143 |doi=10.3847/1538-4357/aba118 |arxiv=2006.15961 |bibcode=2020ApJ...898..143S |doi-access=free }}</ref> or by merger with another black hole.<ref name="PRL-20160211"/>

A small number of candidate black holes with masses in the range {{solar mass|{{value|e=2}}-{{value|e=4}}}} have been reported. These are larger than stellar black holes but smaller than supermassive black holes, are rarer than either extreme, and are called [[intermediate-mass black hole]]s.<ref name="mc04">{{Cite journal |last1=Coleman Miller |first1=M. |last2=Colbert |first2=E. J. M. |title=Intermediate-Mass Black Holes |journal=International Journal of Modern Physics D |date=2004 |volume=13 |issue=1 |pages=1–64 |doi=10.1142/S0218271804004426 |arxiv=astro-ph/0308402 |bibcode=2004IJMPD..13....1M }}</ref> Physicists have speculated that such black holes may form from collisions in [[globular]] and [[star cluster]]s or at the centre of [[dwarf galaxy|low-mass galaxies]].<ref>{{multiref2|{{Cite journal |last1=Coleman Miller |first1=M. |last2=Hamilton |first2=Douglas P. |title=Production of Intermediate-Mass Black Holes in Globular Clusters |journal=Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society |date=2002 |volume=330 |issue=1 |pages=232–240 |doi=10.1046/j.1365-8711.2002.05112.x |arxiv=astro-ph/0106188 |bibcode=2002MNRAS.330..232C |doi-access=free }}|{{Cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=Rizzuto |first1=Francesco Paolo |last2=Naab |first2=Thorsten |last3=Spurzem |first3=Rainer |last4=Giersz |first4=Mirek |last5=Ostriker |first5=J. P. |last6=Stone |first6=N. C. |last7=Wang |first7=Long |last8=Berczik |first8=Peter |last9=Rampp |first9=M. |title=Intermediate Mass Black Hole Formation in Compact Young Massive Star Clusters |journal=Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society |date=2021 |volume=501 |issue=4 |pages=5257–5273 |doi=10.1093/mnras/staa3634 |doi-access=free |arxiv=2008.09571 }}|{{Cite journal |last1=Barai |first1=Paramita |last2=De Gouveia Dal Pino |first2=Elisabete M. |title=Intermediate-Mass Black Hole Growth and Feedback in Dwarf Galaxies at High Redshifts |journal=Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society |date=2019 |volume=487 |issue=4 |pages=5549–5563 |doi=10.1093/mnras/stz1616 |doi-access=free |arxiv=1807.04768 }}}}</ref> They may also form as the result of mergers of smaller black holes, with several gravitational wave measurements consistent with merged black holes within {{solar mass|110–350}}.<ref>{{Cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=Ruiz-Rocha |first1=Krystal |last2=Yelikar |first2=Anjali B. |last3=Lange |first3=Jacob |last4=Gabella |first4=William |last5=Weller |first5=Robert A. |last6=o'Shaughnessy |first6=Richard |last7=Holley-Bockelmann |first7=Kelly |last8=Jani |first8=Karan |title=Properties of "Lite" Intermediate-Mass Black Hole Candidates in LIGO-Virgo's Third Observing Run |journal=The Astrophysical Journal Letters |date=2025 |volume=985 |issue=2 |pages=L37 |doi=10.3847/2041-8213/adc5f8 |arxiv=2502.17681 |bibcode=2025ApJ...985L..37R |doi-access=free }}</ref>

The black holes with the largest masses are called [[supermassive black hole]]s, with masses more than {{solar mass|{{value|e=6}}}}.<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Mezcua |first1=Mar |title=Encyclopedia of Astrobiology |chapter=Black Holes |date=2021 |pages=1–8 |doi=10.1007/978-3-642-27833-4_5510-1 |arxiv=2110.08629 |isbn=978-3-642-27833-4 }}</ref> These black holes are believed to exist at the centres of almost every large galaxy, including the Milky Way.<ref name=Cattaneo-2009/> Some scientists have proposed a subcategory of even larger black holes, called [[ultramassive black hole]]s, with masses greater than {{solar mass|{{value|e=9}}-{{value|e=10}}}}.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Natarajan |first1=Priyamvada |last2=Treister |first2=Ezequiel |title=Is There an Upper Limit to Black Hole Masses? |journal=Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society |date=2009 |volume=393 |issue=3 |pages=838–845 |doi=10.1111/j.1365-2966.2008.13864.x |arxiv=0808.2813 |bibcode=2009MNRAS.393..838N |doi-access=free }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Dullo |first1=Bililign T. |last2=Gil De Paz |first2=Armando |last3=Knapen |first3=Johan H. |title=Ultramassive Black Holes in the Most Massive Galaxies: M <sub>BH</sub>–σ Versus M <sub>BH</sub>–R <sub>b</sub> |journal=The Astrophysical Journal |date=2021 |volume=908 |issue=2 |page=134 |doi=10.3847/1538-4357/abceae |arxiv=2012.04471 |bibcode=2021ApJ...908..134D |doi-access=free }}</ref> Theoretical models predict that the accretion disc that feeds black holes will be unstable once a black hole reaches {{solar mass|{{val|50|e=9}}–{{val|100|e=9}}}}, setting a rough upper limit to black hole mass.<ref>{{cite journal | title=How big can a black hole grow? | last1=King | first1=Andrew | journal=Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society: Letters | volume=456 | issue=1 | pages=L109–L112 | date=February 2016 | doi=10.1093/mnrasl/slv186 | doi-access=free | bibcode=2016MNRAS.456L.109K | arxiv=1511.08502 }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Clery |first1=Daniel |title=Limit to how big black holes can grow is astonishing |journal=Science |date=21 December 2015 |doi=10.1126/science.aae0149 }}</ref>

== Structure ==

While black holes are conceptually invisible sinks of all matter and light, in astronomical settings, their enormous gravity alters the motion of surrounding objects and pulls nearby gas inwards at near-light speed, making the area around black holes the brightest objects in the universe.<ref name="reynolds21">{{cite journal |last1=Reynolds |first1=Christopher S. |title=Observational Constraints on Black Hole Spin |journal=Annual Review of Astronomy and Astrophysics |date=2021 |volume=59 |pages=117–154 |doi=10.1146/annurev-astro-112420-035022 |arxiv=2011.08948 |bibcode=2021ARA&A..59..117R }}</ref>

