{{See also|Astrophysical jet}}
[[File:Black Hole Outflows From Centaurus A.jpg|thumb |alt=See caption. |Relativistic jets from the supermassive black hole in [[Centaurus A]] extend [[perpendicular]]ly from the galaxy.]]
Some black holes have relativistic jets—thin streams of [[plasma (physics)|plasma]] travelling away from the black hole at more than 90% of the speed of light.<ref name="mr99">{{Cite journal |last1=Mirabel |first1=I. F. |last2=Rodríguez |first2=L. F. |title=Sources of Relativistic Jets in the Galaxy |journal=Annual Review of Astronomy and Astrophysics |date=1999 |volume=37 |pages=409–443 |doi=10.1146/annurev.astro.37.1.409 |arxiv=astro-ph/9902062 |bibcode=1999ARA&A..37..409M }}</ref> A small fraction of the matter falling towards the black hole gets accelerated away along the hole rotation axis.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Relativistic Jets |url=https://nustar.caltech.edu/page/relativistic-jets |access-date=9 November 2025 |website=NuSTAR |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260419114356/https://nustar.caltech.edu/page/relativistic-jets |archive-date=19 April 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> These jets can extend as far as millions of [[light-year]]s from the black hole itself.<ref>{{Cite journal |display-authors=2 |last9=Jacob |first9=Joe |last1=Bagchi |first1=Joydeep |last2=Vivek |first2=M. |last3=Vikram |first3=Vinu |last4=Hota |first4=Ananda |last5=Biju |first5=K. G. |last6=Sirothia |first6=S. K. |last7=Srianand |first7=Raghunathan |last8=Gopal-Krishna |title=Megaparsec Relativistic Jets Launched from an Accreting Supermassive Black Hole in an Extreme Spiral Galaxy |journal=The Astrophysical Journal |date=2014 |volume=788 |issue=2 |page=174 |doi=10.1088/0004-637X/788/2/174 |arxiv=1404.6889 |bibcode=2014ApJ...788..174B }}</ref>

Black holes of any mass can have jets.<ref name="nemmen12">{{Cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1= Nemmen|first1= R. S.|last2= Georganopoulos|first2= M.|last3= Guiriec|first3= S.|last4= Meyer|first4= E. T.|last5= Gehrels|first5= N.|last6= Sambruna|first6= R. M.|title=A Universal Scaling for the Energetics of Relativistic Jets from Black Hole Systems|journal= Science|date= 2012|volume= 338|issue= 6113|pages= 1445–1448|doi= 10.1126/science.1227416|pmid= 23239730|arxiv= 1212.3343|bibcode= 2012Sci...338.1445N}}</ref> However, they are typically observed around spinning black holes with strongly-magnetized accretion disks.<ref name="bmr18">{{Cite journal |last1=Blandford |first1=Roger |last2=Meier |first2=David |last3=Readhead |first3=Anthony |title=Relativistic Jets from Active Galactic Nuclei |journal=Annual Review of Astronomy and Astrophysics |date=2019 |volume=57 |pages=467–509 |doi=10.1146/annurev-astro-081817-051948 |arxiv=1812.06025 |bibcode=2019ARA&A..57..467B }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=Chen 陈 |first1=Yongyun 永云 |last2=Gu 顾 |first2=Qiusheng 秋生 |last3=Fan 樊 |first3=Junhui 军辉 |last4=Zhou 周 |first4=Hongyan 宏岩 |last5=Yuan 袁 |first5=Yefei 业飞 |last6=Gu 顾 |first6=Weimin 为民 |last7=Wu 吴 |first7=Qinwen 庆文 |last8=Xiong 熊 |first8=Dingrong 定荣 |last9=Guo 郭 |first9=Xiaotong 晓通 |last10=Ding 丁 |first10=Nan 楠. |last11=Yu 俞 |first11=Xiaoling 效龄 |title=The Powers of Relativistic Jets Depend on the Spin of Accreting Supermassive Black Holes |journal=The Astrophysical Journal |date=2021 |volume=913 |issue=2 |page=93 |doi=10.3847/1538-4357/abf4ff |arxiv=2104.04242 |bibcode=2021ApJ...913...93C |doi-access=free }}</ref> Relativistic jets were more common in the [[Chronology of the universe#Gravity builds cosmic structure|early universe]], when galaxies and their corresponding supermassive black holes were rapidly gaining mass.<ref name="bmr18" /><ref>{{Cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=Ghisellini |first1=G. |last2=Haardt |first2=F. |last3=Ceca |first3=R. Della |last4=Volonteri |first4=M. |last5=Sbarrato |first5=T. |title=The Role of Relativistic Jets in the Heaviest and Most Active Supermassive Black Holes at High Redshift |journal=Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society |date=2013 |volume=432 |issue=4 |pages=2818–2823 |doi=10.1093/mnras/stt637 |doi-access=free |arxiv=1304.1152 }}</ref> All black holes with jets also have an accretion disk,<ref name="mr99" /> but the jets are usually brighter than the disk.<ref>{{Cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=Ghisellini |first1=G. |last2=Tavecchio |first2=F. |last3=Maraschi |first3=L. |last4=Celotti |first4=A. |last5=Sbarrato |first5=T. |title=The Power of Relativistic Jets Is Larger Than the Luminosity of Their Accretion Disks |journal=Nature |date=2014 |volume=515 |issue=7527 |pages=376–378 |doi=10.1038/nature13856 |pmid=25409827 |arxiv=1411.5368 |bibcode=2014Natur.515..376G }}</ref> ''[[Quasar]]s,'' typically found in other galaxies, are believed to be supermassive black holes with jets; ''[[microquasar]]s'' are believed to be stellar-mass objects with jets, typically observed in the Milky Way.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Mirabel |first1=I. F. |last2=Rodríguez |first2=L. F. |title=Microquasars in our Galaxy |journal=Nature |date=1998 |volume=392 |issue=6677 |pages=673–676 |doi=10.1038/33603 |bibcode=1998Natur.392..673M }}</ref>

The jets can be powered by either the accretion disk or the rotating black hole spin. While many details of the jets have been studied, no complete model has emerged.  One method proposed to fuel these jets is the [[Blandford-Znajek process]], which suggests that the dragging of [[magnetic field lines]] by a black hole's rotation could launch jets of matter into space.<ref name="bmr18" /> The [[Penrose process]], which involves extraction of a black hole's [[rotational energy]], has also been proposed as a potential mechanism of jet propulsion.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Penrose |first1=R. |year=1969
|title=Gravitational Collapse: The Role of General Relativity |journal=[[Rivista del Nuovo Cimento]] | volume=1 |pages=252–276 |bibcode=1969NCimR...1..252P}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last1=Narayan |first1=Ramesh |last2=McClintock |first2=Jeffrey E. |last3=Tchekhovskoy |first3=Alexander |title=General Relativity, Cosmology and Astrophysics |chapter=Energy Extraction from Spinning Black Holes Via Relativistic Jets |date=2014 |pages=523–535 |doi=10.1007/978-3-319-06349-2_25 |arxiv=1303.3004 |isbn=978-3-319-06348-5 }}</ref> There is evidence that jets at all scales, from microquasars to [[gamma ray burst]]s may be produced by a single mechanism.<ref name="nemmen12" />

==== Accretion disk ====
{{Main|Accretion disk}}

[[File:Black Hole Desktop & Phone Wallpapers (SVS14146 - BH accretion disk viz desktop).png|thumb|alt=An black hole with an orange-red accretion disk. The disk is wrapped around the black hole in a ring across its center and semicircles around the top and bottom of the black hole. The semicircles are actually the part of the accretion disk that is behind the black hole, and appears gravitationally lensed to be above and below the hole.|Visualization of a black hole with an orange accretion disk. The parts of the disk circling over and under the hole are actually gravitationally lensed from the back side of the black hole.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Black Hole Anatomy |url=https://science.nasa.gov/universe/black-holes/anatomy/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250424044622/https://science.nasa.gov/universe/black-holes/anatomy/ |archive-date=24 April 2025 |access-date=13 October 2025 |website=NASA Science |date=2 August 2022 }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Cunha |first1=Pedro V.P. |last2=Eiró |first2=Nelson A. |last3=Herdeiro |first3=Carlos A.R. |last4=Lemos |first4=José P.S. |title=Lensing and shadow of a black hole surrounded by a heavy accretion disk |journal=Journal of Cosmology and Astroparticle Physics |date=2020 |issue=3 |page=035 |doi=10.1088/1475-7516/2020/03/035 |arxiv=1912.08833 |bibcode=2020JCAP...03..035C }}</ref>]]
Due to [[conservation of angular momentum]], gas falling into the [[gravitational well]] created by a massive object will typically form a disk-like structure around the object.<ref>{{cite book |title=Astrophysics |series=Undergraduate Lecture Notes in Physics |date=2024 |doi=10.1007/978-3-031-22135-4 |isbn=978-3-031-22133-0 }}</ref>{{rp|242}} As the disk's angular momentum is transferred outward due to processes such as turbulence in the disk, its matter falls farther inward, converting its gravitational energy into heat and releasing a large amount of radiation; absent an explosive event, the radiation pressure [[Eddington limit|limits the accretion rate]].<ref name="High-Energy Astrophysics">{{cite book |title=High-Energy Astrophysics |series=Undergraduate Lecture Notes in Physics |date=2022 |doi=10.1007/978-3-030-92159-0 |isbn=978-3-030-92158-3 }}</ref>{{rp|165}} The temperature of these disks can range from thousands to millions of [[kelvin]]s, and temperatures differ throughout a single accretion disk.<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Lasota |first1=Jean-Pierre |title=Astrophysics of Black Holes |chapter=Black Hole Accretion Discs |series=Astrophysics and Space Science Library |date=2016 |volume=440 |pages=1–60 |doi=10.1007/978-3-662-52859-4_1 |arxiv=1505.02172 |isbn=978-3-662-52857-0 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Beloborodov |first1=A. M. |title=Super-Eddington Accretion Discs Around Kerr Black Holes |journal=Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society |date=1998 |volume=297 |issue=3 |pages=739–746 |doi=10.1046/j.1365-8711.1998.01530.x |arxiv=astro-ph/9802129 |bibcode=1998MNRAS.297..739B |doi-access=free }}</ref> Accretion disks radiate across the entire [[electromagnetic spectrum]], depending on the disk's [[turbulence]] and [[magnetisation]] and the black hole's mass and angular momentum.<ref>{{multiref2|{{Cite journal |last1=Page |first1=Don N. |last2=Thorne |first2=Kip S. |title=Disk-Accretion Onto a Black Hole. Time-Averaged Structure of Accretion Disk |journal=The Astrophysical Journal |date=1974 |volume=191 |page=499 |doi=10.1086/152990 |bibcode=1974ApJ...191..499P }}|{{Cite journal |last1=Bisnovatyi-Kogan |first1=Gennady |title=Accretion into Black Hole, And Formation of Magnetically Arrested Accretion Disks |journal=Universe |date=2019 |volume=5 |issue=6 |page=146 |doi=10.3390/universe5060146 |arxiv=1905.13731 |bibcode=2019Univ....5..146B |doi-access=free }}|{{Cite journal |last1=Zakharov |first1=A. F. |last2=Repin |first2=S. V. |title=Model Radiation Spectrum for an Accretion Disk Near a Rotating Black Hole |journal=Astronomy Reports |date=2002 |volume=46 |issue=5 |pages=360–365 |doi=10.1134/1.1479423 |bibcode=2002ARep...46..360Z }}}}</ref>

Accretion disks can be defined as [[geometrical thickness|geometrically]] thin or geometrically thick. Geometrically thin disks are mostly confined to the black hole's equatorial plane and have a well-defined edge at the [[innermost stable circular orbit]] (ISCO), while geometrically thick disks are supported by internal pressure and temperature and can extend inside the ISCO. Disks with high rates of [[electron scattering]] and absorption, appearing bright and [[opaque]], are called ''optically thick''; ''optically thin'' disks are more [[translucent]] and produce fainter images when viewed from afar.<ref name="wang25">{{Cite journal |last1=Wang |first1=Zi-Liang |title=Exploring the Role of Accretion Disk Geometry in Shaping Black Hole Shadows |journal=Physical Review D |date=2025 |volume=112 |issue=6 |article-number=064052 |doi=10.1103/fhqj-wgcm |arxiv=2506.21148 |bibcode=2025PhRvD.112f4052W }}</ref> Accretion disks of black holes accreting beyond the [[Eddington limit]] are often referred to as ''polish donuts'' due to their thick, [[toroid]]al shape that resembles that of a [[donut]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Gimeno-Soler |first1=Sergio |last2=Font |first2=José A. |title=Magnetised Polish Doughnuts Revisited |journal=Astronomy & Astrophysics |date=2017 |volume=607 |pages=A68 |doi=10.1051/0004-6361/201730935  |doi-access=free|arxiv=1707.03867 |bibcode=2017A&A...607A..68G }}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last1=Abramowicz |first1=M.A. |title=Growing Black Holes: Accretion in a Cosmological Context |chapter=Super-Eddington Black Hole Accretion |series=ESO Astrophysics Symposia |date=2005 |pages=257–273 |doi=10.1007/11403913_49 |isbn=978-3-540-25275-7 }}</ref>

Quasar accretion disks are expected to have a "blue spectral shape", meaning that the flux per frequency <math>F_\nu</math> is proportional to <math>\nu^{1/3}</math>; this was not originally observed due to emission from dust surrounding the objects.<ref>{{Cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=Kishimoto |first1=Makoto |last2=Antonucci |first2=Robert |last3=Blaes |first3=Omer |last4=Lawrence |first4=Andy |last5=Boisson |first5=Catherine |last6=Albrecht |first6=Marcus |last7=Leipski |first7=Christian |title=The Characteristic Blue Spectra of Accretion Disks in Quasars as Uncovered in the Infrared |journal=Nature |date=2008 |volume=454 |issue=7203 |pages=492–494 |doi=10.1038/nature07114 |pmid=18650919 |arxiv=0807.3703 |bibcode=2008Natur.454..492K }}</ref> The disk for a stellar black hole, on the other hand, would likely look orange, yellow, or red, with its inner regions being the brightest.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Fukue |first1=Jun |last2=Yokoyama |first2=Takushi |title=Color Photographs of an Accretion Disk Around a Black Hole |journal=Publications of the Astronomical Society of Japan |date=1988 |volume=40 |pages=15–24 |doi=10.1093/pasj/40.1.15}}</ref> Accretion disk colours may also be altered by the [[Doppler effect]], with the part of the disk travelling towards an observer appearing bluer and brighter and the part of the disk travelling away from the observer appearing redder and dimmer.<ref name="jtft15">{{Cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=James |first1=Oliver |last2=Tunzelmann |first2=Eugénie von |last3=Franklin |first3=Paul |last4=Thorne |first4=Kip S. |title=Gravitational Lensing by Spinning Black Holes in Astrophysics, And in the Movie ''Interstellar'' |journal=Classical and Quantum Gravity |date=2015 |volume=32 |issue=6 |doi=10.1088/0264-9381/32/6/065001 |arxiv=1502.03808 |bibcode=2015CQGra..32f5001J }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=Guo |first1=Sen |last2=Huang |first2=Yu-Xiang |last3=Cui |first3=Yu-Hao |last4=Han |first4=Yan |last5=Jiang |first5=Qing-Quan |last6=Liang |first6=En-Wei |last7=Lin |first7=Kai |title=Unveiling the Unconventional Optical Signatures of Regular Black Holes Within Accretion Disk |journal=The European Physical Journal C |date=2023 |volume=83 |issue=11 |article-number=1059 |doi=10.1140/epjc/s10052-023-12208-0  |doi-access=free|arxiv=2310.20523 |bibcode=2023EPJC...83.1059G }}</ref>

==== Innermost stable circular orbit (ISCO) ====
{{Main|Innermost stable circular orbit}}

[[File:How to Measure the Spin of a Black Hole.jpg|thumb|alt=Artistic depiction of three black holes, showing that the accretion disk is closer to the black hole if it is orbiting in the same direction that the black hole is rotating. Graphs next to these images show changes in the x-ray spectra released by the disks.|Since particles in a black hole's accretion disk must orbit at or outside the ISCO, astronomers can observe the properties of accretion disks to determine black hole spins.<ref>{{Cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=McClintock |first1=Jeffrey E. |last2=Narayan |first2=Ramesh |last3=Davis |first3=Shane W. |last4=Gou |first4=Lijun |last5=Kulkarni |first5=Akshay |last6=Orosz |first6=Jerome A. |last7=Penna |first7=Robert F. |last8=Remillard |first8=Ronald A. |last9=Steiner |first9=James F. |title=Measuring the Spins of Accreting Black Holes |journal=Classical and Quantum Gravity |date=2011 |volume=28 |issue=11 |doi=10.1088/0264-9381/28/11/114009 |arxiv=1101.0811 |bibcode=2011CQGra..28k4009M }}</ref>]]
In [[Newtonian gravity]], [[test particle]]s can stably orbit at arbitrary distances from a central object. In general relativity, however, there exists a smallest possible radius for which a massive particle can orbit stably. Any infinitesimal inward [[perturbation (astronomy)|perturbations]] to this orbit will lead to the particle [[orbital decay|spiralling into]] the black hole, and any outward perturbations will, depending on the energy, cause the particle to spiral in, move to a stable orbit further from the black hole, or escape to infinity. This orbit is called the '''innermost stable circular orbit''', or ISCO.<ref name="Misner-1973">{{cite book |last1=Misner |first1=Charles |last2=Thorne |first2=Kip S. |last3=Wheeler |first3=John |author1-link=Charles W. Misner |author2-link=Kip Thorne |author3-link=John Archibald Wheeler |title=Gravitation |publisher=W. H. Freeman and Company |date=1973 |isbn=978-0-7167-0344-0}}</ref> In the case of a Schwarzschild black hole (spin zero) and a particle without [[spin (physics)|spin]], the location of the ISCO is:
<math display="block">r_{\rm ISCO}=3 \, r_\text{s}=\frac{6 \, GM}{c^2},</math>where <math>r_{\rm_{ISCO}}</math> is the radius of the ISCO, <math>r_\text{s}</math> is the Schwarzschild radius of the black hole, <math>G</math> is the gravitational constant, and <math>c</math> is the speed of light.<ref name="Bardeen1972" /> For spinning black holes, the ISCO is moved inwards for particles orbiting in the same direction that the black hole is spinning ([[retrograde and prograde motion|prograde]]) and outwards for particles orbiting in the opposite direction (retrograde). For example, the ISCO for a particle orbiting retrograde can be as far out as about <math>4.5r_\text{s}</math>, while the ISCO for a particle orbiting prograde can be as close as at the event horizon itself. The radius of this orbit changes slightly based on particle spin.<ref name="jtb15">{{Cite journal |last1=Jefremov |first1=Paul I. |last2=Tsupko |first2=Oleg Yu. |last3=Bisnovatyi-Kogan |first3=Gennady S. |title=Innermost Stable Circular Orbits of Spinning Test Particles in Schwarzschild and Kerr Space-Times |journal=Physical Review D |date=2015 |volume=91 |issue=12 |article-number=124030 |doi=10.1103/PhysRevD.91.124030 |arxiv=1503.07060 |bibcode=2015PhRvD..91l4030J }}</ref>
For charged black holes, the ISCO moves inwards.<ref name="zwgsl18">{{Cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=Zhang |first1=Yu-Peng |last2=Wei |first2=Shao-Wen |last3=Guo |first3=Wen-Di |last4=Sui |first4=Tao-Tao |last5=Liu |first5=Yu-Xiao |title=Innermost Stable Circular Orbit of Spinning Particle in Charged Spinning Black Hole Background |journal=Physical Review D |date=2018 |volume=97 |issue=8 |article-number=084056 |doi=10.1103/PhysRevD.97.084056 |arxiv=1711.09361 |bibcode=2018PhRvD..97h4056Z }}</ref>

==== Photon sphere and shadow ====
{{anchor|Photon sphere}}
{{Main|Photon sphere}}

[[File:Photon capture by a Schwarzschild black hole - video.ogg|thumb|thumbtime=6|Video of a photon being captured by a Schwarzschild black hole |alt=A photon (small, black with yellow trail) being captured in the [[photon sphere]] of a Schwarzschild black hole (large, black, center)]]

The [[photon sphere]] is a spherical boundary for which photons moving on tangents to that sphere are bent completely around the black hole, possibly orbiting multiple times.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Vázquez |first1=S.E. |last2=Esteban |first2=E.P. |title=Strong-field gravitational lensing by a Kerr black hole |journal=Il Nuovo Cimento B |date=2004 |volume=119 |issue=5 |page=489 |doi=10.1393/ncb/i2004-10121-y |arxiv=gr-qc/0308023 |bibcode=2004NCimB.119..489V }}</ref> For Schwarzschild black holes, the photon sphere has a radius 1.5 times the Schwarzschild radius.<ref name="ll19">{{Cite journal |last1=Lü |first1=H. |last2=Lyu |first2=Hong-Da |title=Schwarzschild Black Holes Have the Largest Size |journal=Physical Review D |date=2020 |volume=101 |issue=4 |article-number=044059 |doi=10.1103/PhysRevD.101.044059 |arxiv=1911.02019 |bibcode=2020PhRvD.101d4059L }}</ref><ref name="qiao22">{{Cite journal |last1=Qiao |first1=Chen-Kai |title=Curvatures, Photon Spheres, And Black Hole Shadows |journal=Physical Review D |date=2022 |volume=106 |issue=8 |article-number=084060 |doi=10.1103/PhysRevD.106.084060 |arxiv=2208.01771 |bibcode=2022PhRvD.106h4060Q }}</ref>

Although light can still escape from the photon sphere, any light that crosses it on an inward trajectory is inevitably captured by the black hole. Consequently, any light reaching a distant observer from the photon sphere must have been emitted by objects located between the photon sphere and the event horizon.<ref name="prd84_6" /> Light emitted towards the photon sphere may also curve around the black hole and return to the emitter.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Cramer |first1=Claes R. |title=Using the Uncharged Kerr Black Hole as a Gravitational Mirror |journal=General Relativity and Gravitation |date=1997 |volume=29 |issue=4 |pages=445–454 |doi=10.1023/A:1018878515046 |arxiv=gr-qc/9510053 |bibcode=1997GReGr..29..445C }}</ref>

For a rotating, uncharged black hole, the radius of the photon sphere depends on the spin parameter and whether the photon is orbiting prograde or retrograde.<ref name="Bardeen1972">{{cite journal|last1=Bardeen|first1=James M.|last2=Press|first2=William H.|last3=Teukolsky|first3=Saul A.|date=1 December 1972|title=Rotating Black Holes: Locally Nonrotating Frames, Energy Extraction, And Scalar Synchrotron Radiation|journal=The Astrophysical Journal|volume=178|pages=347–370|doi=10.1086/151796|bibcode=1972ApJ...178..347B}}</ref> For a photon orbiting prograde, the photon sphere will be 0.5–1.5 Schwarzschild radii from the centre of the black hole, while for a photon orbiting retrograde, the photon sphere will be between 3–4 Schwarzschild radii from the centre of the black hole. The exact locations of the photon spheres depend on the [[magnitude (mathematics)#numbers|magnitude]] of the black hole's rotation.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Teo |first1=Edward |title=Spherical Photon Orbits Around a Kerr Black Hole |journal=General Relativity and Gravitation |date=2003 |volume=35 |issue=11 |pages=1909–1926 |doi=10.1023/A:1026286607562 |bibcode=2003GReGr..35.1909T |url=http://scholarbank.nus.edu.sg/handle/10635/97985 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240720165441/https://scholarbank.nus.edu.sg/handle/10635/97985 |archive-date=20 July 2024 |access-date=29 October 2025 |url-status=live }}</ref> For a charged, nonrotating black hole, there will only be one photon sphere, and the radius of the photon sphere will decrease for increasing black hole charge.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Heydarzade |first1=Yaghoub |last2=Vertogradov |first2=Vitalii |title=Dynamical Photon Spheres in Charged Black Holes and Naked Singularities |journal=The European Physical Journal C |date=2024 |volume=84 |issue=6 |article-number=582 |doi=10.1140/epjc/s10052-024-12945-w  |doi-access=free|arxiv=2311.08930 |bibcode=2024EPJC...84..582H }}</ref> For non-[[extremal black hole|extremal]], charged, rotating black holes, there will always be two photon spheres, with the exact radii depending on the parameters of the black hole.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Chen |first1=Ying-Xuan |last2=Huang |first2=Jia-Hui |last3=Jiang |first3=Haoxiang |title=Radii of Spherical Photon Orbits Around Kerr-Newman Black Holes |journal=Physical Review D |date=2023 |volume=107 |issue=4 |article-number=044066 |doi=10.1103/PhysRevD.107.044066 |arxiv=2210.08509 |bibcode=2023PhRvD.107d4066C }}</ref>

When viewed from a great distance, the photon sphere creates an observable '''black hole shadow,''' a dark silhouette of the black hole against the background stars.{{anchor|Black hole shadow}} Images such as those taken by the Event Horizon Telescope show the black hole shadow, not the event horizon itself.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Perlick |first1=Volker |last2=Tsupko |first2=Oleg Yu. |title=Calculating black hole shadows: Review of analytical studies |journal=Physics Reports |date=February 2022 |volume=947 |pages=1–39 |doi=10.1016/j.physrep.2021.10.004 |arxiv=2105.07101 |bibcode=2022PhR...947....1P }}</ref> Since no light emerges from within the black hole, this shadow is the limit for possible observations.<ref name="High-Energy Astrophysics"/>{{rp|152}} The shadow of colliding black holes should have characteristic warped shapes, allowing scientists to detect black holes that are about to merge.<ref name="prd84_6">{{cite journal |last1=Nitta |first1=Daisuke |last2=Chiba |first2=Takeshi |last3=Sugiyama |first3=Naoshi |title=Shadows of Colliding Black Holes |journal=Physical Review D |volume=84 |issue=6 |article-number=063008 |date=September 2011 |doi=10.1103/PhysRevD.84.063008 |bibcode=2011PhRvD..84f3008N |arxiv=1106.2425 }}</ref>

==== Ergosphere ====
{{Main|Ergosphere}}

[[File:Ergosphere and event horizon of a rotating black hole (no animation).gif|thumb|alt=The ergosphere as a peanut-shaped region that touches the event horizon in the middle and then bulges outwards at the poles. The black hole depicted has a spin 99% of the maximum.|The ergosphere is a region outside of the event horizon, where objects cannot remain in place.<ref name="Visser-2007" />]]

Near a rotating black hole, spacetime rotates similar to a vortex. The rotating spacetime will drag any matter and light into rotation around the spinning black hole. This effect of general relativity, called [[frame dragging]], gets stronger closer to the spinning mass. The region of spacetime in which it is impossible to stay still is called the ergosphere.<ref name="reynolds19" />

The ergosphere of a black hole is the region of space bounded by the event horizon and the ergosurface, also known as the ''stationary limit surface''. The ergosurface coincides with the event horizon at the poles but bulges outward around the equator.<ref name="Visser-2007" />

Matter and radiation can escape from the ergosphere. Through the [[Penrose process]], objects can emerge from the ergosphere with more energy than they entered with. The extra energy is taken from the rotational energy of the black hole, slowing down the rotation of the black hole.<ref name="Carroll-2019">{{cite book |last1=Carroll |first1=Sean M. |title=Spacetime and Geometry |date=2019 |doi=10.1017/9781108770385 |isbn=978-1-108-48839-6 }}</ref>{{rp|268}}

==== Plunging region ====
{{Main|Plunging region}}

The observable region of spacetime surrounding a black hole closest to its event horizon is known as the plunging region. Within this region, free-falling matter can no longer maintain stable circular orbits or halt its final descent into the black hole. Instead, it rapidly plunges inward at speeds approaching the speed of light, becoming increasingly hot and producing a distinctive, detectable [[thermal radiation|thermal emission]].<ref>{{cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=Mummery |first1=Andrew |last2=Ingram |first2=Adam |last3=Davis |first3=Shane |last4=Fabian |first4=Andrew |title=Continuum Emission from Within the Plunging Region of Black Hole Discs |journal=Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society |volume=531 |issue=1 |pages=366–386 |date=June 2024 |doi=10.1093/mnras/stae1160|doi-access=free |arxiv=2405.09175 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Machida |first1=Mami |last2=Matsumoto |first2=Ryoji |title=Global Three-Dimensional Magnetohydrodynamic Simulations of Black Hole Accretion Disks: X-Ray Flares in the Plunging Region |journal=The Astrophysical Journal |date=2003 |volume=585 |issue=1 |pages=429–442 |doi=10.1086/346070 |arxiv=astro-ph/0211240 |bibcode=2003ApJ...585..429M }}</ref> However, light and radiation emitted from this region can still escape from the black hole's gravitational pull.<ref name="prisco">{{Cite web |title=Study Proves Black Holes Have a 'Plunging Region,' Just as Einstein Predicted |url=https://www.cnn.com/2024/05/17/world/black-holes-einstein-plunging-region-scn |last=Prisco |first=Jacopo |website=[[CNN]] |date=17 May 2024 |access-date=29 October 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251113193018/https://www.cnn.com/2024/05/17/world/black-holes-einstein-plunging-region-scn |archive-date=13 November 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Radius ===
For a nonspinning, uncharged black hole, the radius of the event horizon, or Schwarzschild radius, is proportional to the mass, ''M'', through
<math display="block">r_\mathrm{s}=\frac{2GM}{c^2} \approx 2.95\, \frac{M}{M_\odot}~\mathrm{km,}</math>
where ''r''{{sub|s}} is the Schwarzschild radius, ''G'' is the [[gravitational constant]], ''c'' is the [[speed of light]], and {{solar mass}} is the [[mass of the Sun]].<ref>{{cite book |last=Wald |first=Robert M. |author-link=Robert Wald |title=General Relativity |publisher=University of Chicago Press |date=1984 |isbn=978-0-226-87033-5 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=9S-hzg6-moYC |access-date=23 February 2016 |archive-date=11 August 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160811035125/https://books.google.com/books?id=9S-hzg6-moYC |url-status=live }}</ref>{{rp|124}} A black hole of the same mass with nonzero spin has two radii:<ref name=Zee-2013/>{{rp|467}}
<math display="block">r_{\pm} = M \pm \sqrt{M^2 - {{(J/M)}}^2}.</math>
Most observed black holes have close to maximum angular momentum that seems to be allowed: <math>|J| \le M^2.</math> For such black holes the radii will approach<ref name=Zee-2013/>{{rp|467}}
<math display="block">r_\mathrm{+}=\frac{GM}{c^2}.</math>
Since the volume within the Schwarzschild radius increases with the cube of the radius, average density of a black hole inside its Schwarzschild radius is inversely proportional to the square of its mass: supermassive black holes are much less dense than stellar black holes. The average density of a {{Solar mass|{{10^|8}}}} black hole is comparable to that of water.<ref name="CMS1999" />

=== Event horizon ===
{{Main|Event horizon}}

{{multiple image
| align     = right
| direction = vertical
| width     = 300
| image1    = BH-no-escape-1.svg
| alt1      = A light cone near the event horizon of a black hole with arrows coming out of it to show the directions that a particle there can move. It is far enough away from the event horizon that that it is not warped towards the event horizon and a particle there can move in any direction.
| caption1  = Far away from the black hole, a particle can move in any direction, as illustrated by the set of arrows. It is restricted only by the speed of light.
| image2    = BH-no-escape-2.svg
| alt2      = A light cone very close to the event horizon of a black hole, with arrows coming out of it to show the directions that a particle there can move. It is bent towards the event horizon, with more arrows going towards the event horizon than away, but a particle can still travel away from the event horizon.
| caption2  = Closer to the black hole, spacetime starts to deform. There are more paths going towards the black hole than paths moving away.
| image3    = BH-no-escape-3.svg
| caption3  = Inside of the event horizon, all paths bring the particle closer to the centre of the black hole. It is no longer possible for the particle to escape. (The diagram is a "cartoon" version of an [[Eddington–Finkelstein coordinates]] diagram; in other coordinates the light cones are not tilted in this way.<ref name="Misner-1973" />{{rp|p=848}})
| alt3      = A light cone inside of the event horizon of a black hole, with arrows coming out of it to show the directions that a particle there can move. All of the arrows lead further inside the event horizon, with none leading outwards.
}}

The defining feature of a black hole is the existence of an event horizon, a boundary in [[spacetime]] through which matter and light can pass only inward towards the centre of the black hole. Nothing, not even light, can escape from inside the event horizon.<ref>{{cite book |title=The New Physics |edition=illustrated |first1=Paul |last1=Davies |publisher=Cambridge University Press |year=1992 |isbn=978-0-521-43831-5 |page=26 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=akb2FpZSGnMC |access-date=25 September 2020 |archive-date=17 August 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210817161727/https://books.google.com/books?id=akb2FpZSGnMC |url-status=live }} [https://books.google.com/books?id=akb2FpZSGnMC&pg=PA26 Extract of page 26] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210815222341/https://books.google.com/books?id=akb2FpZSGnMC&pg=PA26 |date=15 August 2021 }}</ref><ref>{{cite book |title=A Student's Guide to the Mathematics of Astronomy |edition=illustrated |first1=Daniel |last1=Fleisch |first2=Julia |last2=Kregenow |publisher=Cambridge University Press |year=2013 |isbn=978-1-107-03494-5 |page=168 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=x4gaBQAAQBAJ |access-date=25 September 2020 |archive-date=17 August 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210817045139/https://books.google.com/books?id=x4gaBQAAQBAJ |url-status=live }} [https://books.google.com/books?id=x4gaBQAAQBAJ&pg=PA168 Extract of page 168] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210817113029/https://books.google.be/books?id=x4gaBQAAQBAJ&pg=PA168 |date=17 August 2021 }}</ref> The event horizon is referred to as such because if an event occurs within the boundary, information from that event cannot reach or affect an outside observer, making it impossible to determine whether such an event occurred.<ref name="JCWheeler-2007" />{{rp|179}}
For non-rotating black holes, the geometry of the event horizon is precisely spherical, while for rotating black holes, the event horizon is [[oblate spheroid|oblate]].<ref name="Wiltshire2009">{{cite book |last1=Visser |first1=Matt |chapter=The Kerr spacetime – a brief introduction |pages=3–37 |editor1-last=Wiltshire |editor1-first=David L. |editor2-last=Visser |editor2-first=Matt |editor3-last=Scott |editor3-first=Susan |title=The Kerr Spacetime: Rotating Black Holes in General Relativity |date=2009 |publisher=Cambridge University Press |isbn=978-0-521-88512-6 }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Delgado |first1=Jorge F. M. |last2=Herdeiro |first2=Carlos A. R. |last3=Radu |first3=Eugen |title=Horizon geometry for Kerr black holes with synchronized hair |journal=Physical Review D |date=2018 |volume=97 |issue=12 |article-number=124012 |doi=10.1103/PhysRevD.97.124012 |bibcode=2018PhRvD..97l4012D |arxiv=1804.04910 }}</ref><ref name="Visser-2007">{{cite arXiv |last1=Visser |first1=Matt |title=The Kerr spacetime: A brief introduction |date=2007 |class=gr-qc |eprint=0706.0622 }}</ref>

To a distant observer, a clock near a black hole would appear to tick more slowly than one further from the black hole.<ref name="Carroll-2004" />{{rp|p=217}}<ref name="scienceofinterstellar" /> This effect, known as [[gravitational time dilation]], would also cause an object falling into a black hole to appear to slow as it approached the event horizon, never quite reaching the horizon from the perspective of an outside observer.<ref name="Carroll-2004" />{{rp|p=218}} All processes on this object would appear to slow down, and any light emitted by the object to appear redder and dimmer, an effect known as [[gravitational redshift]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://nrumiano.free.fr/Estars/int_bh.html |title=Inside a Black Hole |website=Knowing the universe and its secrets |access-date=26 March 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090423053437/http://nrumiano.free.fr/Estars/int_bh.html |archive-date=23 April 2009 }}</ref> An object falling from half of a Schwarzschild radius above the event horizon would fade away until it could no longer be seen, disappearing from view within one hundredth of a second for an {{val|8|u=solar mass}} black hole.<ref>{{cite web |title=What Happens to You If You Fall into a Black Hole |url=http://math.ucr.edu/home/baez/physics/Relativity/BlackHoles/fall_in.html |website=math.ucr.edu |publisher=[[John Baez]] | access-date=11 March 2018 |archive-date=13 February 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190213124648/http://math.ucr.edu/home/baez/physics/Relativity/BlackHoles/fall_in.html |url-status=live }}</ref> It would also appear to flatten onto the black hole, joining all other material that had ever fallen into the hole.<ref>{{Cite magazine |date=1 April 1997 |title=Black Holes and the Information Paradox |magazine=Scientific American |last=Susskind |first=Leonard |pages=52–57 |issue=April 1997 |author-link=Leonard Susskind |jstor=24993702 }}</ref>

On the other hand, an observer falling into a black hole would not notice any of these effects as they cross the event horizon. Their own clocks appear to them to tick normally, and they cross the event horizon after a finite time without noting any singular behaviour.<ref name="HamiltonA">{{cite web|author=Hamilton, A.|url=http://jila.colorado.edu/~ajsh/insidebh/schw.html|title=Journey into a Schwarzschild black hole|website=jila.colorado.edu|access-date=28 June 2020|archive-date=3 September 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190903235853/https://jila.colorado.edu/~ajsh/insidebh/schw.html|url-status=live}}</ref> In [[general relativity]], it is impossible to determine the location of the event horizon from local observations, due to Einstein's [[equivalence principle]].<ref name="Carroll-2004" />{{rp|p=222}}

=== Internal geometry ===
==== Cauchy horizon ====
{{Main|Cauchy horizon}}

Black holes that are rotating or charged have an inner horizon, often called the Cauchy horizon, inside of the black hole.<ref name="pi90">{{Cite journal |last1=Poisson |first1=Eric |last2=Israel |first2=Werner |title=Internal Structure of Black Holes |journal=Physical Review D |date=1990 |volume=41 |issue=6 |pages=1796–1809 |doi=10.1103/PhysRevD.41.1796 |pmid=10012548 |bibcode=1990PhRvD..41.1796P }}</ref> The inner horizon is divided up into two segments: an ingoing section and an outgoing section.<ref name="st14">{{Cite journal |last1=Scheel |first1=M. A. |last2=Thorne |first2=K. S. |title=Geometrodynamics: The Nonlinear Dynamics of Curved Spacetime |journal=Physics-Uspekhi |date=2014 |volume=57 |issue=4 |pages=342–351 |doi=10.3367/UFNe.0184.201404b.0367 |arxiv=1706.09078 |bibcode=2014PhyU...57..342S }}</ref>

At the ingoing section of the Cauchy horizon, radiation and matter that fall into the black hole would build up at the horizon, causing the curvature of spacetime to go to infinity. This would cause an observer falling in to experience tidal forces.<ref name="pi90" /><ref name="st14" /> This phenomenon is often called [[mass inflation]], since it is associated with a [[parameter]] dictating the black hole's internal mass [[exponential growth|growing exponentially]],<ref name="pi90" /><ref name="mo12">{{Cite journal |last1=Marolf |first1=Donald |last2=Ori |first2=Amos |title=Outgoing Gravitational Shock Wave at the Inner Horizon: The Late-Time Limit of Black Hole Interiors |journal=Physical Review D |date=2012 |volume=86 |issue=12 |article-number=124026 |doi=10.1103/PhysRevD.86.124026 |arxiv=1109.5139 |bibcode=2012PhRvD..86l4026M }}</ref> and the buildup of tidal forces is called the mass-inflation singularity<ref name="ori91">{{Cite journal |last1=Ori |first1=Amos |title=Inner Structure of a Charged Black Hole: An Exact Mass-Inflation Solution |journal=Physical Review Letters |date=1991 |volume=67 |issue=7 |pages=789–792 |doi=10.1103/PhysRevLett.67.789 |pmid=10044989 |bibcode=1991PhRvL..67..789O }}</ref><ref name="st14" /> or Cauchy horizon singularity.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Burko |first1=Lior M. |title=Structure of the Black Hole's Cauchy-Horizon Singularity |journal=Physical Review Letters |date=1997 |volume=79 |issue=25 |pages=4958–4961 |doi=10.1103/PhysRevLett.79.4958 |arxiv=gr-qc/9710112 |bibcode=1997PhRvL..79.4958B }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Burko |first1=Lior M. |last2=Khanna |first2=Gaurav |last3=Zenginoǧlu |first3=Anıl |title=Cauchy-Horizon Singularity Inside Perturbed Kerr Black Holes |journal=Physical Review D |date=2016 |volume=93 |issue=4 |article-number=041501 |doi=10.1103/PhysRevD.93.041501 |arxiv=1601.05120 |bibcode=2016PhRvD..93d1501B }}</ref> Some physicists have argued that in realistic black holes, accretion and Hawking radiation would stop mass inflation from occurring.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Hamilton |first1=Andrew J. S. |title=Mass Inflation Followed by Belinskii-Khalatnikov-Lifshitz Collapse Inside Accreting, Rotating Black Holes |journal=Physical Review D |date=2017 |volume=96 |issue=8 |article-number=084041 |doi=10.1103/PhysRevD.96.084041 |arxiv=1703.01921 |bibcode=2017PhRvD..96h4041H }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=Barceló |first1=Carlos |last2=Boyanov |first2=Valentin |last3=Carballo-Rubio |first3=Raúl |last4=Garay |first4=Luis J. |title=Classical Mass Inflation Versus Semiclassical Inner Horizon Inflation |journal=Physical Review D |date=2022 |volume=106 |issue=12 |article-number=124006 |doi=10.1103/PhysRevD.106.124006 |arxiv=2203.13539 |bibcode=2022PhRvD.106l4006B }}</ref>

At the outgoing section of the inner horizon, infalling radiation would [[backscatter]] off of the black hole's spacetime curvature and travel outward, building up at the outgoing Cauchy horizon. This would cause an infalling observer to experience a gravitational [[shock wave]] and tidal forces as the spacetime curvature at the horizon grew to infinity. This buildup of tidal forces is called the [[shock singularity]].<ref name="mo12" /><ref name="st14" />

In the [[Penrose-Hawking singularity theorem]], both of these singularities are weak, meaning that an object crossing them would only be deformed a finite amount by tidal forces, even though the spacetime curvature would still be infinite at the singularity. This is as opposed to a strong singularity, where an object hitting the singularity would be stretched and squeezed by an infinite amount.<ref name="ori91" /><ref name="mo12" /> They are also null singularities, meaning that a photon could travel [[parallel (geometry)|parallel]] to them without ever being intercepted.<ref name="st14" />

==== Singularity ====
{{Main|Gravitational singularity}}

Ignoring quantum effects, every black hole has a singularity inside, points where the curvature of spacetime becomes infinite, and [[geodesic]]s terminate within a finite [[proper time]].<ref name="Carroll-2004" />{{rp|p=205}}<ref name="hp70">{{Cite journal |last1=Hawking |first1=S. W. |last2=Penrose |first2=R. |title=The Singularities of Gravitational Collapse and Cosmology |journal=Proceedings of the Royal Society of London. A. Mathematical and Physical Sciences |date=1970 |volume=314 |issue=1519 |pages=529–548 |doi=10.1098/rspa.1970.0021 |bibcode=1970RSPSA.314..529H }}</ref> For a non-rotating black hole, this region takes the shape of a single point; for a rotating black hole it is smeared out to form a [[ring singularity]] that lies in the plane of rotation.<ref name="Carroll-2004" />{{rp|p=264}} In both cases, the singular region has zero volume. All of the mass of the black hole ends up in the singularity.<ref name="Carroll-2004" />{{rp|p=252}} Since the singularity has nonzero mass in an infinitely small space, it can be thought of as having infinite [[density]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Sizes of Black Holes? How Big Is a Black Hole? |url=https://www.skyandtelescope.com/astronomy-resources/how-big-is-a-black-hole/ |access-date=9 October 2018 |work=[[Sky & Telescope]] | date=22 July 2014 |archive-date=3 April 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190403035741/https://www.skyandtelescope.com/astronomy-resources/how-big-is-a-black-hole/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

[[File:BKL dynamics - video.ogg|thumbtime=0|thumb|alt=A sphere being continuously warped into different ellipsoids, with the direction of the warping constantly changing.|An object approaching a gravitational singularity will experience chaotic oscillations of spacetime.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Of Singularities And Breadmaking |url=https://www.einstein-online.info/en/spotlight/singularities_bkl/ |last=Garfinkle |first=David |access-date=28 April 2026 |website=Einstein Online |year=2007 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250709022836/https://www.einstein-online.info/en/spotlight/singularities_bkl/ |archive-date=9 July 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>]]

Observers falling into a Schwarzschild black hole (i.e., non-rotating and not charged) cannot avoid being carried into the singularity once they cross the event horizon.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Lewis |first1=Geraint F. |last2=Kwan |first2=Juliana |title=No Way Back: Maximizing Survival Time Below the Schwarzschild Event Horizon |journal=Publications of the Astronomical Society of Australia |date=2007 |volume=24 |issue=2 |pages=46–52 |doi=10.1071/AS07012 |arxiv=0705.1029 |bibcode=2007PASA...24...46L }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Toporensky |first1=Alexei |last2=Popov |first2=Sergei |title=How to Delay Death and Look Further into the Future if You Fall into a Black Hole |journal=Resonance |date=2023 |volume=28 |issue=5 |pages=737–749 |doi=10.1007/s12045-023-1602-8 }}</ref> As they fall further into the black hole, they will be torn apart by the growing [[tidal force]]s in a process sometimes referred to as [[spaghettification]] or the ''noodle effect''. Eventually, they will reach the singularity and be crushed into an infinitely small point.<ref name="JCWheeler-2007">{{cite book |last=Wheeler |first=J. Craig | author-link = J. Craig Wheeler |title=Cosmic Catastrophes |edition=2nd |publisher=Cambridge University Press |date=2007 |isbn=978-0-521-85714-7}}</ref>{{rp|p=182}} However, any perturbations, such as those caused by matter or radiation falling in, would cause space to [[BKL singularity|oscillate chaotically]] near the singularity. Any matter falling in would experience intense tidal forces rapidly changing in direction, all while being compressed into an increasingly small volume.<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Belinskii |first1=V.A. |last2=Lifshitz |first2=E.M. |last3=Khalatnikov |first3=I.M. |last4=Agyei |first4=A.K. |title=Perspectives in Theoretical Physics |chapter=The oscillatory mode of approach to a singularity in homogeneous cosmological models with rotating axes |date=1992 |pages=677–689 |doi=10.1016/B978-0-08-036364-6.50048-X |isbn=978-0-08-036364-6 }}</ref><ref name="scienceofinterstellar">{{Cite book |title=The Science of Interstellar |last=Thorne |first=Kip |date=7 November 2014 |publisher=W. W. Norton & Company |isbn=978-0-393-35137-8}}</ref>{{rp|231}}

Alternative forms of general relativity, including addition of some quantum effects, can lead to ''regular'', or ''nonsingular'', black holes without singularities.<ref>{{Cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=Lan |first1=Chen |last2=Yang |first2=Hao |last3=Guo |first3=Yang |last4=Miao |first4=Yan-Gang |title=Regular Black Holes: A Short Topic Review |journal=International Journal of Theoretical Physics |date=2023 |volume=62 |issue=9 |article-number=202 |doi=10.1007/s10773-023-05454-1 |arxiv=2303.11696 |bibcode=2023IJTP...62..202L }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Olmo |first1=Gonzalo |last2=Rubiera-Garcia |first2=Diego |title=Nonsingular Black Holes in ƒ (R) Theories |journal=Universe |date=2015 |volume=1 |issue=2 |pages=173–185 |doi=10.3390/universe1020173 |arxiv=1509.02430 |bibcode=2015Univ....1..173O |doi-access=free }}</ref> For example, the [[Fuzzball (string theory)|fuzzball]] model, based on [[string theory]], states that black holes are actually made up of [[quantum state|quantum microstates]] and need not have a singularity or an event horizon.<ref>{{cite journal |last=Mathur |first=Samir D. |title=The Fuzzball Proposal for Black Holes: An Elementary Review |journal=Fortschritte der Physik |volume=53 |issue=7–8 |page=793 |date=2005 |doi=10.1002/prop.200410203 |arxiv=hep-th/0502050 |bibcode=2005ForPh..53..793M }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Avery |first1=Steven G. |last2=Chowdhury |first2=Borun D. |last3=Puhm |first3=Andrea |title=Unitarity and Fuzzball Complementarity: "Alice Fuzzes but May Not Even Know It!" |journal=Journal of High Energy Physics |date=2013 |issue=9 |article-number=12 |doi=10.1007/JHEP09(2013)012 |arxiv=1210.6996 |bibcode=2013JHEP...09..012A }}</ref> The theory of [[loop quantum gravity]] proposes that the curvature and density at the centre of a black hole is large, but not infinite.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Bojowald |first1=Martin |title=Black-Hole Models in Loop Quantum Gravity |journal=Universe |date=2020 |volume=6 |issue=8 |page=125 |doi=10.3390/universe6080125 |arxiv=2009.13565 |bibcode=2020Univ....6..125B |doi-access=free }}</ref>

== Formation ==
Black holes are formed by [[gravitational collapse]] of massive stars, either by direct collapse or during a supernova explosion in a process called ''fallback''.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Woosley |first1=S. E. |last2=Heger |first2=A. |last3=Weaver |first3=T. A. |title=The evolution and explosion of massive stars |journal=Reviews of Modern Physics |date=2002 |volume=74 |issue=4 |pages=1015–1071 |doi=10.1103/RevModPhys.74.1015 |bibcode=2002RvMP...74.1015W }}</ref> Black holes can result from the merger of two [[neutron star]]s or a neutron star and a black hole.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Zappa |first1=Francesco |last2=Bernuzzi |first2=Sebastiano |last3=Pannarale |first3=Francesco |last4=Mapelli |first4=Michela |last5=Giacobbo |first5=Nicola |title=Black-Hole Remnants from Black-Hole–Neutron-Star Mergers |journal=Physical Review Letters |date=2019 |volume=123 |issue=4 |article-number=041102 |doi=10.1103/PhysRevLett.123.041102 |pmid=31491270 |arxiv=1903.11622 |bibcode=2019PhRvL.123d1102Z }}</ref> Other more speculative mechanisms include [[primordial black hole]]s created from density fluctuations in the early universe, the collapse of [[Dark star (Newtonian mechanics)|dark stars]], a hypothetical object powered by annihilation of [[dark matter]], or from hypothetical [[self-interacting dark matter]].<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Inayoshi |first1=Kohei |last2=Visbal |first2=Eli |last3=Haiman |first3=Zoltán |title=The Assembly of the First Massive Black Holes |journal=Annual Review of Astronomy and Astrophysics |date=2020 |volume=58 |pages=27–97 |doi=10.1146/annurev-astro-120419-014455 |arxiv=1911.05791 |bibcode=2020ARA&A..58...27I }}</ref>

=== Supernova ===
Gravitational collapse occurs when an object's internal [[pressure]] is insufficient to resist the object's own gravity. At the end of a star's life, it will run out of [[hydrogen]] to [[nuclear fusion|fuse]], and will start fusing more and more massive elements, until it gets to [[iron]]. Since the fusion of elements heavier than iron would [[endothermic reaction|require more energy than it would release]], nuclear fusion ceases. If the iron core of the star is too massive, the star will no longer be able to support itself and will undergo gravitational collapse.<ref>{{Cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=Janka |first1=H. |last2=Langanke |first2=K. |last3=Marek |first3=A. |last4=Martinezpinedo |first4=G. |last5=Muller |first5=B. |title=Theory of Core-Collapse Supernovae |journal=Physics Reports |date=2007 |volume=442 |issue=1–6 |pages=38–74 |doi=10.1016/j.physrep.2007.02.002 |arxiv=astro-ph/0612072 |bibcode=2007PhR...442...38J }}</ref>

The mass of a black hole formed via a supernova has a lower bound: if the progenitor star is too small, the collapse may be stopped by the [[Degenerate matter|degeneracy pressure]] of the star's constituents, allowing the condensation of matter into an exotic denser state. Degeneracy pressure occurs from the [[Pauli exclusion principle]]: particles will resist being in the same place as each other. Progenitor stars with masses less than about {{Solar mass|8}} will become [[white dwarf]]s, where the degeneracy pressure of electrons balances gravity. For more massive progenitor stars, the force of gravity overcomes electron degeneracy pressure and the star compresses until [[neutron degeneracy pressure]] resists gravity, forming a [[neutron star]]. If the star is even more massive, neutron degeneracy pressure will not be able to resist the force of gravity and the star will collapse into a black hole.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Bennett |first1=Jeffrey |title=Degeneracy Pressure in Stars and Stellar Corpses |journal=The Physics Teacher |date=2025 |volume=63 |issue=3 |pages=212–213 |doi=10.1119/5.0260882 |bibcode=2025PhTea..63c.212B }}</ref><ref name="Carroll-2004">{{cite book |last1=Carroll |first1=Sean M. |author-link=Sean M. Carroll |title=Spacetime and Geometry: An Introduction to General Relativity |title-link=Spacetime and Geometry |publisher=Addison-Wesley |year=2003 |isbn=978-0-8053-8732-2}}, the lecture notes on which the book was based are available for free from Sean Carroll's [https://www.preposterousuniverse.com/spacetimeandgeometry/ website] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170323013522/http://www.preposterousuniverse.com/spacetimeandgeometry/|date=23 March 2017}}</ref>{{rp|loc=5.8}}

While most of the energy released during gravitational collapse is emitted very quickly, an outside observer does not actually see the end of this process. Even though the collapse takes a finite amount of time from the [[reference frame]] of infalling matter, a distant observer would see the infalling material slow and halt just above the event horizon, due to gravitational time dilation. Light from the collapsing material takes longer and longer to reach the observer, with the delay growing to infinity as the emitting material reaches the event horizon. Thus the external observer never sees the formation of the event horizon; instead, the collapsing material seems to become dimmer and increasingly red-shifted, eventually fading away.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Penrose |first1=R. |title="Golden Oldie": Gravitational Collapse: The Role of General Relativity |journal=General Relativity and Gravitation |date=2002 |volume=34 |issue=7 |pages=1141–1165 |doi=10.1023/A:1016578408204 |bibcode=2002GReGr..34.1141P }}</ref>

=== Other mechanisms ===
Observations of quasars from less than a billion years after the [[Big Bang]]<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Bañados |first1=Eduardo |last2=Venemans |first2=Bram P. |last3=Mazzucchelli |first3=Chiara |last4=Farina |first4=Emanuele P. |last5=Walter |first5=Fabian |last6=Wang |first6=Feige |last7=Decarli |first7=Roberto |last8=Stern |first8=Daniel |last9=Fan |first9=Xiaohui |last10=Davies |first10=Frederick B. |last11=Hennawi |first11=Joseph F. |last12=Simcoe |first12=Robert A. |last13=Turner |first13=Monica L. |last14=Rix |first14=Hans-Walter |last15=Yang |first15=Jinyi |last16=Kelson |first16=Daniel D. |last17=Rudie |first17=Gwen C. |last18=Winters |first18=Jan Martin |display-authors=2 |title=An 800-million-solar-mass black hole in a significantly neutral Universe at a redshift of 7.5 |journal=Nature |date=2018 |volume=553 |issue=7689 |pages=473–476 |doi=10.1038/nature25180 |pmid=29211709 |arxiv=1712.01860 |bibcode=2018Natur.553..473B }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Boylan-Kolchin |first1=Michael |last2=Weisz |first2=Daniel R. |title=Uncertain Times: The Redshift–Time Relation from Cosmology and Stars |journal=Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society |date=2021 |volume=505 |issue=2 |pages=2764–2783 |doi=10.1093/mnras/stab1521 |doi-access=free |arxiv=2103.15825 }}</ref> has led to investigations of other ways to form black holes. The accretion process to build supermassive black holes has a limiting rate of mass accumulation and a billion years is not enough time to reach quasar status. One suggestion is [[Direct collapse black hole|direct collapse]] of nearly pure hydrogen gas (low metalicity) clouds characteristic of the young universe, forming a supermassive star which collapses into a black hole. It has been suggested that seed black holes with typical masses of ~{{Solar mass|{{10^|5}}}} could have formed in this way which then could grow to ~{{Solar mass|{{10^|9}}}}. However, the very large amount of gas required for direct collapse is not typically stable against fragmentation which would form multiple stars. Thus another approach suggests massive star formation followed by collisions that seed massive black holes which ultimately merge to create a quasar.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Klessen |first1=Ralf S. |last2=Glover |first2=Simon C.O. |title=The First Stars: Formation, Properties, and Impact |journal=Annual Review of Astronomy and Astrophysics |date=2023 |volume=61 |pages=65–130 |doi=10.1146/annurev-astro-071221-053453 |arxiv=2303.12500 |bibcode=2023ARA&A..61...65K }}</ref>{{rp|85}}

A neutron star in a [[common envelope]] with a regular star can accrete sufficient material to collapse to a black hole or two neutron stars can merge. These avenues for the formation of black holes are considered relatively rare.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Fryer |first1=Chris L. |last2=Kalogera |first2=Vassiliki |title=Theoretical Black Hole Mass Distributions |journal=The Astrophysical Journal |date=2001 |volume=554 |issue=1 |pages=548–560 |doi=10.1086/321359 |arxiv=astro-ph/9911312 |bibcode=2001ApJ...554..548F }}</ref>

=== Primordial black holes and the Big Bang ===
In the early universe, fluctuations of spacetime may have formed areas that were denser than their surroundings. Rapid early expansion of the universe may have allowed these areas to collapse, forming [[primordial black hole]]s.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Primordial Black Holes |url=https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/14524/ |last=Balzer |first=Ashley |date=7 May 2024 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250827012141/https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/14524/ |archive-date=27 August 2025 |access-date=23 November 2025 |website=NASA SVS}}</ref> Various models predict the creation of primordial black holes ranging from a [[Planck mass]] (~{{val|2.2|e=-8|u=kg}}) to hundreds of thousands of solar masses. Primordial black holes with masses less than {{val|e=12|u=kg}} would have evaporated by now due to Hawking radiation.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Sasaki |first1=Misao |last2=Suyama |first2=Teruaki |last3=Tanaka |first3=Takahiro |last4=Yokoyama |first4=Shuichiro |title=Primordial black holes—perspectives in gravitational wave astronomy |journal=Classical and Quantum Gravity |date=2018 |volume=35 |issue=6 |doi=10.1088/1361-6382/aaa7b4 |arxiv=1801.05235 |bibcode=2018CQGra..35f3001S }}</ref>

Despite the early universe being extremely [[dense]], it did not re-collapse into a black hole during the Big Bang, since the universe was expanding rapidly and did not have the gravitational differential necessary for black hole formation. Models for the gravitational collapse of objects of relatively constant size, such as [[star]]s, do not necessarily apply in the same way to rapidly expanding space such as the Big Bang.<ref>{{multiref2|{{cite web |author1=Philip Gibbs |title=Is the Big Bang a Black Hole? |url=http://math.ucr.edu/home/baez/physics/Relativity/BlackHoles/universe.html |publisher=[[John Baez]] | access-date=16 March 2018 |archive-date=31 December 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181231021714/http://math.ucr.edu/home/baez/physics/Relativity/BlackHoles/universe.html |url-status=live }}|{{Cite web |title=Why Didn't the Infant Universe Collapse into a Black Hole? |url=https://www.space.com/why-infant-universe-not-collapse-black-hole |last=Sutter |first=Paul |date=21 June 2023 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250325152015/https://www.space.com/why-infant-universe-not-collapse-black-hole |archive-date=25 March 2025 |access-date=24 November 2025 |website=Space.com}}|{{Cite web |title=According to the Big Bang Theory, All the Matter in the Universe Erupted from a Singularity. Why Didn't All This Matter—Cheek by Jowl as It Was—Immediately Collapse into a Black Hole? |url=https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/according-to-the-big-bang/ |last=Musser |first=George |date=22 September 2003 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250426033551/https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/according-to-the-big-bang/ |archive-date=26 April 2025 |access-date=24 November 2025 |website=Scientific American}}}}</ref>

=== High-energy collisions ===
In principle, black holes could be formed in [[high-energy physics|high-energy]] particle collisions that achieve sufficient density, although no such events have been detected.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Park |first1=Seong Chan |title=Black holes and the LHC: A review |journal=Progress in Particle and Nuclear Physics |date=July 2012 |volume=67 |issue=3 |pages=617–650 |doi=10.1016/j.ppnp.2012.03.004 |arxiv=1203.4683 |bibcode=2012PrPNP..67..617P }}</ref>
These hypothetical [[micro black hole]]s, which could form from the collision of [[cosmic ray]]s and Earth's atmosphere or in [[particle accelerator]]s like the [[Large Hadron Collider]], would not be able to aggregate additional mass.<ref name="LHCsafety">{{cite journal |last1=Ellis |first1=John |last2=Giudice |first2=Gian |last3=Mangano |first3=Michelangelo |last4=Tkachev |first4=Igor |last5=Wiedemann |first5=Urs |author6=LHC Safety Assessment Group |title=Review of the safety of LHC collisions |journal=Journal of Physics G: Nuclear and Particle Physics |date=2008 |volume=35 |issue=11 |doi=10.1088/0954-3899/35/11/115004 |arxiv=0806.3414 |bibcode=2008JPhG...35k5004E }}</ref> Instead, they would [[black hole evaporation|evaporate]] in about 10{{sup|−25}} seconds, posing no threat to the Earth.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Peskin |first1=M. E. |title=The End of the World at the Large Hadron Collider? |journal=Physics |volume=1 |article-number=14 |date=2008 |doi=10.1103/Physics.1.14 |bibcode=2008PhyOJ...1...14P|doi-access=free}}</ref>

== Evolution ==
After a black hole forms, it may change through phenomena such as [[black hole merger|mergers]], [[accretion (astrophysics)|accretion of matter]], and evaporation via [[Hawking radiation]].

=== Merger ===
[[File:BBH gravitational lensing of gw150914.webm|alt=A simulation of two black holes colliding to form a super massive black hole|thumb|Simulation of two black holes colliding]]

Black holes can merge with other objects such as stars or [[binary black hole|other black holes]]. This is thought to have been important, especially in the early growth of supermassive black holes, which could have formed from the aggregation of many smaller objects.<ref name="ReesVolonteri">{{cite conference |first1=M. J. |series=Proceedings of the International Astronomical Union |last1=Rees |first2=M. |last2=Volonteri |author2-link=Marta Volonteri|title=Massive Black Holes: Formation and Evolution |editor1-first=V. |editor1-last=Karas |editor2-first=G. |editor2-last=Matt |book-title=Black Holes from Stars to Galaxies—Across the Range of Masses |pages=51–58 |date=2007 |isbn=978-0-521-86347-6 |arxiv=astro-ph/0701512 |bibcode=2007IAUS..238...51R |doi=10.1017/S1743921307004681 |doi-access=free }}</ref> The process has also been proposed as the origin of some intermediate-mass black holes.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Zwart |first1=S. F. P. |last2=Baumgardt |first2=H. |last3=Hut |first3=P. |last4=Makino |first4=J. |last5=McMillan |first5=S. L. W. |display-authors=2 |title=Formation of Massive Black Holes Through Runaway Collisions in Dense Young Star Clusters |journal=Nature |volume=428 |issue=6984 |date=2004 |doi=10.1038/nature02448 |pmid=15085124 |arxiv=astro-ph/0402622 |bibcode=2004Natur.428..724P |pages=724–726 }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=O'Leary |first1=R. M. |last2=Rasio |first2=F. A. |last3=Fregeau |first3=J. M. |last4=Ivanova |first4=N. |last5=o'Shaughnessy |first5=R. |title=Binary Mergers and Growth of Black Holes in Dense Star Clusters |journal=The Astrophysical Journal |volume=637 |issue=2 |pages=937–951 |date=2006 |doi=10.1086/498446 |arxiv=astro-ph/0508224 |bibcode=2006ApJ...637..937O }}</ref> Mergers of supermassive black holes may take a long time: As a binary of supermassive black holes approach each other, most nearby stars are [[gravitational slingshot|slingshotted]] away, leaving little for the black holes to gravitationally interact with that would allow them to get closer to each other. This phenomenon has been called the [[final parsec problem]], as the distance at which this happens is usually around one [[parsec]].<ref>{{Cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=Ryu |first1=Taeho |last2=Perna |first2=Rosalba |last3=Haiman |first3=Zoltán |last4=Ostriker |first4=Jeremiah P. |last5=Stone |first5=Nicholas C. |title=Interactions Between Multiple Supermassive Black Holes in Galactic Nuclei: A Solution to the Final Parsec Problem |journal=Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society |date=2018 |volume=473 |issue=3 |pages=3410–3433 |doi=10.1093/mnras/stx2524 |doi-access=free |arxiv=1709.06501 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Vasiliev |first1=Eugene |last2=Antonini |first2=Fabio |last3=Merritt |first3=David |title=The Final-Parsec Problem in Nonspherical Galaxies Revisited |journal=The Astrophysical Journal |date=2014 |volume=785 |issue=2 |page=163 |doi=10.1088/0004-637X/785/2/163 |arxiv=1311.1167 |bibcode=2014ApJ...785..163V }}</ref>

=== Accretion of matter ===
{{See also|Accretion disk}}
[[File:Centaurus A- A Nearby Elliptical Galaxy With An Active Galactic Nucleus (2001-0157blue - 0157blue xray).tiff|thumb|alt=An image, all blue, of Centaurus A's active galactic nucleus as a bright spot in the center with a bright relativistic jet going away from it.|The active galactic nucleus of galaxy [[Centaurus A]] in X-ray light, believed to be powered by a supermassive black hole (centre) and surrounded by x-ray binaries (blue dots)]]

When a black hole [[accretion (astrophysics)|accretes]] matter, the gas in the inner accretion disk orbits at very high speeds because of its proximity to the black hole. This fast-moving gas experiences [[friction]] against the slower-moving gas in the outer disk, transferring [[angular momentum]] to the outer disk. The loss of angular momentum forces the gas in the inner disk to orbit closer to the black hole, and its [[gravitational potential energy]] is converted into [[thermal energy]]. This heats the inner disk to temperatures at which it emits vast amounts of electromagnetic radiation (mainly [[X-ray]]s), which is detectable by telescopes. By the time the matter of the disk reaches the [[Innermost stable circular orbit|ISCO]], between 5.7% and 42% of its [[mass-energy equivalence|mass will have been converted to energy]], depending on the black hole's spin. About 90% of this energy is released within 20 black hole radii.<ref name="McClintockRemillard2006">{{cite book |last1=McClintock |first1=J. E. |last2=Remillard |first2=R. A. |chapter=Black Hole Binaries |editor1-first=W. |editor1-last=Lewin|editor2-first=M. |editor2-last=van der Klis |title=Compact Stellar X-Ray Sources |page=157 |date=2006 |isbn=978-0-521-82659-4 |arxiv=astro-ph/0306213 |bibcode=2006csxs.book..157M}} section 4.1.5.</ref> In many cases, accretion disks are accompanied by [[relativistic jets]] that are emitted along the black hole's poles, which carry away much of the energy.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Kuroda |first1=Takami |last2=Shibata |first2=Masaru |title=Numerical Relativity Simulations of Black Hole and Relativistic Jet Formation |journal=Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society: Letters |date=2024 |volume=533 |pages=L107–L112 |doi=10.1093/mnrasl/slae069 |doi-access=free |arxiv=2404.02792 }}</ref>

Many of the universe's most energetic phenomena have been attributed to the accretion of matter on black holes. [[Active galactic nuclei]] and [[quasar]]s are powered by accretion onto supermassive black holes.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Saikia |first1=D. J. |title=Jets in Radio Galaxies and Quasars: An Observational Perspective |journal=Journal of Astrophysics and Astronomy |date=2022 |volume=43 |issue=2 |article-number=97 |doi=10.1007/s12036-022-09863-2 |arxiv=2206.05803 |bibcode=2022JApA...43...97S }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=Czerny |first1=Bożena |last2=Cao |first2=Shulei |last3=Jaiswal |first3=Vikram Kumar |last4=Karas |first4=Vladimír |last5=Khadka |first5=Narayan |last6=Martínez-Aldama |first6=Mary Loli |last7=Naddaf |first7=Mohammad Hassan |last8=Panda |first8=Swayamtrupta |last9=Pozo Nuñez |first9=Francisco |last10=Prince |first10=Raj |last11=Ratra |first11=Bharat |last12=Sniegowska |first12=Marzena |last13=Yu |first13=Zhefu |last14=Zajaček |first14=Michal |title=Accretion Disks, Quasars and Cosmology: Meandering Towards Understanding |journal=Astrophysics and Space Science |date=2023 |volume=368 |issue=2 |article-number=8 |doi=10.1007/s10509-023-04165-7 |arxiv=2209.06563 |bibcode=2023Ap&SS.368....8C }}</ref> [[X-ray binaries]] are generally accepted to be [[binary star|binary]] systems in which one of the two objects is a [[compact object]] accreting matter from its companion.<ref name="CMS1999" /> [[Ultraluminous X-ray source]]s may be the accretion disks of intermediate-mass black holes.<ref>{{cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=Winter |first1=L. M. |last2=Mushotzky |first2=R. F. |last3=Reynolds |first3=C. S. |title=XMM-Newton Archival Study of the Ultraluminous X-Ray Population in Nearby Galaxies |date=2006 |journal=The Astrophysical Journal |volume=649 |issue=2 |pages=730–752 |arxiv=astro-ph/0512480 |doi=10.1086/506579 |bibcode=2006ApJ...649..730W }}</ref>

At a certain rate of accretion, the outward radiation pressure will become as strong as the inward gravitational force, and the black hole should, in theory, be unable to accrete any faster. This limit is called the [[Eddington limit]]. Realistically, many black holes accrete beyond this rate due to their non-spherical geometry or instabilities in the accretion disk. Accretion beyond the limit is called [[super-Eddington accretion]] and may have been commonplace in the early universe.<ref>{{Cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=Brightman |first1=M. |last2=Bachetti |first2=M. |last3=Earnshaw |first3=H. P. |last4=Fürst |first4=F. |last5=García |first5=J. |last6=Grefenstette |first6=B. |last7=Heida |first7=M. |last8=Kara |first8=E. |last9=Madsen |first9=K. K. |last10=Middleton |first10=M. J. |last11=Stern |first11=D. |last12=Tombesi |first12=F. |last13=Walton |first13=D. J. |title=Breaking the Limit: Super-Eddington Accretion Onto Black Holes and Neutron Stars |journal=Bulletin of the American Astronomical Society |date=2019 |volume=51 |issue=3 |page=352 |arxiv=1903.06844 |bibcode=2019BAAS...51c.352B }}</ref><ref name="Regan">{{Cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=Regan |first1=John A. |last2=Downes |first2=Turlough P. |last3=Volonteri |first3=Marta |last4=Beckmann |first4=Ricarda |last5=Lupi |first5=Alessandro |last6=Trebitsch |first6=Maxime |last7=Dubois |first7=Yohan |title=Super-Eddington Accretion and Feedback from the First Massive Seed Black Holes |journal=Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society |date=2019 |volume=486 |issue=3 |pages=3892–3906 |doi=10.1093/mnras/stz1045 |doi-access=free |arxiv=1811.04953 }}</ref>

Stars have been observed to get torn apart by tidal forces in the immediate vicinity of supermassive black holes in galaxy nuclei, in what is known as a [[tidal disruption event]] (TDE). Some of the material from the disrupted star forms an accretion disk around the black hole, which emits observable electromagnetic radiation.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Evans |first1=Charles R. |last2=Kochanek |first2=Christopher S. |title=The Tidal Disruption of a Star by a Massive Black Hole |journal=The Astrophysical Journal |date=1989 |volume=346 |pages=L13 |doi=10.1086/185567 |bibcode=1989ApJ...346L..13E }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Komossa |first1=S. |title=Tidal Disruption of Stars by Supermassive Black Holes: Status of Observations |journal=Journal of High Energy Astrophysics |date=2015 |volume=7 |pages=148–157 |doi=10.1016/j.jheap.2015.04.006 |arxiv=1505.01093 |bibcode=2015JHEAp...7..148K }}</ref>

=== Interaction with galaxies ===
The correlation between the masses of supermassive black holes in the centres of galaxies with the [[M-sigma relation|velocity dispersion]] and mass of stars in their [[galactic bulge|host bulges]] suggests that the formation of galaxies and the formation of their central black holes are related. Black hole [[cosmic wind|winds]] from rapid accretion, particularly when the galaxy itself is still accreting matter, can compress gas nearby, accelerating star formation. However, if the winds become too strong, the black hole may blow nearly all of the gas out of the galaxy, quenching star formation. Black hole jets may also energise nearby [[stellar-wind bubble|cavities]] of plasma and eject low-[[entropy]] gas from out of the galactic core, causing gas in galactic centres to be [[Cooling flow#Cooling flow problem|hotter than expected]].<ref name=Cattaneo-2009>{{Cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=Cattaneo |first1=A. |last2=Faber |first2=S. M. |last3=Binney |first3=J. |last4=Dekel |first4=A. |last5=Kormendy |first5=J. |last6=Mushotzky |first6=R. |last7=Babul |first7=A. |last8=Best |first8=P. N. |last9=Brüggen |first9=M. |last10=Fabian |first10=A. C. |last11=Frenk |first11=C. S. |last12=Khalatyan |first12=A. |last13=Netzer |first13=H. |last14=Mahdavi |first14=A. |last15=Silk |first15=J. |last16=Steinmetz |first16=M. |last17=Wisotzki |first17=L. |title=The Role of Black Holes in Galaxy Formation and Evolution |journal=Nature |date=2009 |volume=460 |issue=7252 |pages=213–219 |doi=10.1038/nature08135 |pmid=19587763 |arxiv=0907.1608 |bibcode=2009Natur.460..213C }}</ref>

=== Evaporation ===
{{Main|Hawking radiation}}

If Hawking's theory of black hole radiation is correct, then black holes are expected to shrink and evaporate over time as they lose mass by the emission of photons and other particles.<ref name=Carlip-2015/>{{rp|420}} The temperature of this thermal spectrum ([[Hawking temperature]]) is proportional to the surface gravity of the black hole, which is inversely proportional to the mass. Hence, large black holes emit less radiation than small black holes.<ref name="Carroll-2004" />{{rp|loc=Ch. 9.6}}<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Ruiz |first1=O. |last2=Molina |first2=U. |last3=Viloria |first3=P. |title=Thermodynamic Analysis of Kerr-Newman Black Holes |journal=Journal of Physics: Conference Series |date=2019 |volume=1219 |issue=1 |article-number=012016 |doi=10.1088/1742-6596/1219/1/012016 |bibcode=2019JPhCS1219a2016R |quote=From this, an expression is established for the Hawking temperature of a Kerr-Newman black hole as a function of its mass 𝑀, angular moment 𝐽 and load 𝑄. As the black hole loses mass, its temperature increases inversely proportional.|hdl=11323/6160 |hdl-access=free }}</ref> A stellar black hole of {{Solar mass|1}} has a Hawking temperature of 62&nbsp;[[nanokelvin]]s.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Siegel |first1=Ethan |author-link1=Ethan Siegel |title=Ask Ethan: Do Black Holes Grow Faster Than They Evaporate? |url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/startswithabang/2017/08/19/ask-ethan-do-black-holes-grow-faster-than-they-evaporate/ |access-date=17 March 2018 |work=Forbes ("Starts With A Bang" blog) |date=2017 |archive-date=22 November 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181122031830/https://www.forbes.com/sites/startswithabang/2017/08/19/ask-ethan-do-black-holes-grow-faster-than-they-evaporate/ |url-status=live }}</ref> This is far less than the 2.7&nbsp;K temperature of the [[cosmic microwave background]] radiation. Stellar-mass or larger black holes receive more mass from the cosmic microwave background than they emit through Hawking radiation and thus will grow instead of shrinking.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Sivaram |first1=C. |title=Black Hole Hawking Radiation May Never Be Observed! |journal=General Relativity and Gravitation |date=2001 |volume=33 |issue=2 |pages=175–181 |bibcode=2001GReGr..33..175S |doi=10.1023/A:1002753400430 }}</ref> To have a Hawking temperature larger than 2.7&nbsp;K (and be able to evaporate), a black hole would need a mass less than the [[Moon]]. Such a black hole would have a diameter of less than a tenth of a millimetre.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.einstein-online.info/elementary/quantum/evaporating_bh/?set_language=en |title=Evaporating Black Holes? |website=Einstein online |publisher=Max Planck Institute for Gravitational Physics |date=2010 |access-date=12 December 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110722055345/http://www.einstein-online.info/elementary/quantum/evaporating_bh/?set_language=en |archive-date=22 July 2011}}</ref>

The Hawking radiation for an astrophysical black hole is predicted to be very weak and would thus be exceedingly difficult to detect from Earth. A possible exception is the [[microsecond]]-long burst of [[gamma ray]]s emitted in the last stage of the evaporation of primordial black holes. Extensive searches for such radiation have proven unsuccessful and provide upper limits on the possibility of existence of low mass primordial black holes.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Auffinger |first1=Jérémy |title=Primordial black hole constraints with Hawking radiation—A review |journal=Progress in Particle and Nuclear Physics |date=July 2023 |volume=131 |article-number=104040 |doi=10.1016/j.ppnp.2023.104040 |arxiv=2206.02672 |bibcode=2023PrPNP.13104040A }}</ref>

=== Laws of mechanics and thermodynamics ===
{{Main|Black hole thermodynamics}}

[[File:Bekenstein-Hawking entropy of a black hole.svg|thumb|alt=A geodesic sphere made up of triangles labelled as the black hole event horizon. Each triangle is labelled as "one Planck area" and "one unit of entropy".|A black hole's entropy [[Direct proportionality|scales]] with the surface area of its event horizon.]]
When based in general relativity, the constraints on a black hole's properties are called the [[laws of black hole mechanics]]. For a black hole that is not still forming or accreting matter, the zeroth law of black hole mechanics states the black hole's [[surface gravity]] is constant across the event horizon. The first law relates changes in the black hole's surface area, angular momentum, and charge to changes in its energy. The second law says the surface area of a black hole never decreases on its own. Finally, the third law says that the surface gravity of a black hole is never zero. These laws are mathematical analogues of the [[laws of thermodynamics]]. They are not equivalent, however, because, according to general relativity without quantum mechanics, a black hole can never emit radiation, and thus its temperature must always be zero.<ref name="WaldLiving">{{cite journal |last=Wald |first=Robert M. |date=2001 |title=The Thermodynamics of Black Holes |journal=Living Reviews in Relativity |volume=4 |issue=1 |article-number=6 |doi=10.12942/lrr-2001-6 |doi-access=free |pmc=5253844 |pmid=28163633 |arxiv=gr-qc/9912119 |bibcode=2001LRR.....4....6W }}</ref>{{rp|11}}<ref name="carlip14">{{Cite journal |last1=Carlip |first1=S. |title=Black Hole Thermodynamics |journal=International Journal of Modern Physics D |date=2014 |volume=23 |issue=11 |pages=1430023–1430736 |doi=10.1142/S0218271814300237 |arxiv=1410.1486 |bibcode=2014IJMPD..2330023C }}</ref>

Quantum mechanics predicts that a black hole will continuously emit thermal Hawking radiation, and therefore must always have a nonzero temperature. It also predicts that all black holes have [[entropy]] which scales with their surface area. When quantum mechanics is accounted for, the laws of black hole mechanics become equivalent to the classical laws of thermodynamics.<ref name="WaldLiving" /><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Witten |first1=Edward |title=Introduction to Black Hole Thermodynamics |journal=The European Physical Journal Plus |date=2025 |volume=140 |issue=5 |article-number=430 |doi=10.1140/epjp/s13360-025-06288-y |arxiv=2412.16795 |bibcode=2025EPJP..140..430W }}</ref> However, these conclusions are derived without a complete theory of quantum gravity, although many potential theories do predict black holes having entropy and temperature. Thus, the true quantum nature of black hole thermodynamics continues to be debated.<ref name="WaldLiving" />{{rp|29}}<ref name="carlip14" />

== Observational evidence ==
Millions of black holes derived from stellar collapse are expected to exist in the [[Milky Way]]. Even a [[dwarf galaxy]] like [[Draco (dwarf galaxy)|Draco]] should have hundreds.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Elbert |first1=Oliver D. |last2=Bullock |first2=James S. |last3=Kaplinghat |first3=Manoj |title=Counting black holes: The cosmic stellar remnant population and implications for LIGO |journal=Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society |date=2018 |volume=473 |pages=1186–1194 |doi=10.1093/mnras/stx1959 |doi-access=free |arxiv=1703.02551 }}</ref> Only a few of these have been detected.
By nature, black holes do not themselves emit any electromagnetic radiation other than the hypothetical, typically extremely weak Hawking radiation, so astrophysicists searching for black holes must rely on indirect observations. The defining characteristic of a black hole is its event horizon. The horizon itself cannot be imaged,<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Abramowicz |first1=M. A. |last2=Kluźniak |first2=W. |last3=Lasota |first3=J.-P. |title=No observational proof of the black-hole event-horizon |journal=Astronomy & Astrophysics |date=2002 |volume=396 |issue=3 |pages=L31–L34 |doi=10.1051/0004-6361:20021645  |doi-access=free|arxiv=astro-ph/0207270 |bibcode=2002A&A...396L..31A }}</ref> so all other possible explanations for these indirect observations must be considered and eliminated before concluding that a black hole has been observed.<ref>{{Cite journal |display-authors=1 |last1=Event Horizon Telescope Collaboration |last2=Akiyama |first2=Kazunori |last3=Alberdi |first3=Antxon |last4=Alef |first4=Walter |last5=Algaba |first5=Juan Carlos |last6=Anantua |first6=Richard |last7=Asada |first7=Keiichi |last8=Azulay |first8=Rebecca |last9=Bach |first9=Uwe |last10=Baczko |first10=Anne-Kathrin |last11=Ball |first11=David |last12=Baloković |first12=Mislav |last13=Barrett |first13=John |last14=Bauböck |first14=Michi |last15=Benson |first15=Bradford A. |date=May 2022 |title=First Sagittarius A* Event Horizon Telescope Results. VI. Testing the Black Hole Metric |journal=The Astrophysical Journal Letters |volume=930 |issue=2 |pages=L17 |doi=10.3847/2041-8213/ac6756 |doi-access=free |arxiv=2311.09484 |bibcode=2022ApJ...930L..17E }}</ref>{{rp|11}}

