The [[Event Horizon Telescope]] (EHT) is a global system of radio telescopes capable of directly observing a black hole shadow.<ref name="APJL-20190410" /> The [[angular resolution]] of a telescope is based on its [[aperture]] and the wavelengths it is observing. Because the [[angular diameter]]s of Sagittarius A* and Messier 87* in the sky are very small, a single telescope would need to be about the size of the Earth to clearly distinguish their horizons using radio wavelengths. By combining data from several different radio telescopes around the world, the Event Horizon Telescope creates an effective aperture the diameter size of the Earth. The EHT team used [[CLEAN (algorithm)|imaging algorithms]] to compute the most probable image from the data in its [[History of black hole physics#EHT|observations of Sagittarius A* and M87*]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Akiyama |first1=Kazunori |last2=Alberdi |first2=Antxon |last3=Alef |first3=Walter |last4=Asada |first4=Keiichi |last5=Azulay |first5=Rebecca |last6=Baczko |first6=Anne-Kathrin |last7=Ball |first7=David |last8=Baloković |first8=Mislav |last9=Barrett |first9=John |last10=Bintley |first10=Dan |last11=Blackburn |first11=Lindy |last12=Boland |first12=Wilfred |last13=Bouman |first13=Katherine L. |last14=Bower |first14=Geoffrey C. |last15=Bremer |first15=Michael |last16=Brinkerink |first16=Christiaan D. |last17=Brissenden |first17=Roger |last18=Britzen |first18=Silke |last19=Broderick |first19=Avery E. |last20=Broguiere |first20=Dominique |last21=Bronzwaer |first21=Thomas |last22=Byun |first22=Do-Young |last23=Carlstrom |first23=John E. |last24=Chael |first24=Andrew |last25=Chan |first25=Chi-Kwan |last26=Chatterjee |first26=Shami |last27=Chatterjee |first27=Koushik |last28=Chen |first28=Ming-Tang |last29=Chen 陈 |first29=Yongjun 永军 |last30=Cho |first30=Ilje |display-authors=2 |title=First M87 Event Horizon Telescope Results. II. Array and Instrumentation |journal=The Astrophysical Journal Letters |date=2019 |volume=875 |issue=1 |pages=L2 |doi=10.3847/2041-8213/ab0c96 |doi-access=free |arxiv=1906.11239 |bibcode=2019ApJ...875L...2E }}</ref><ref name="eht19" />

=== Gravitational waves ===
[[Gravitational-wave observatory|Gravitational-wave interferometry]] can be used to detect merging black holes and other compact objects. In this method, a laser beam is split, sent down two long arms of a tunnel, then reflected at the far end of the tunnels to reconverge at the intersection of the arms, precisely [[destructive interference|cancelling each other]]. However, when a gravitational wave passes, it warps spacetime, changing the relative lengths of the arms themselves. Since each laser beam is now travelling a slightly different distance, they do not cancel out and produce a recognisable signal. Analysis of the signal can give scientists information about what caused the gravitational waves. Since gravitational waves are very weak, gravitational-wave observatories such as [[LIGO]] must have arms several kilometres long and carefully control for [[seismic noise|noise]] from Earth to be able to detect these gravitational waves.<ref>{{Cite web |title=FAQ |url=https://www.ligo.caltech.edu/page/faq |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260113010624/https://www.ligo.caltech.edu/page/faq |archive-date=13 January 2026 |access-date=5 February 2026 |website=LIGO Lab}}</ref> Since [[History of black hole physics#LIGO|the first measurements in 2016]], multiple gravitational waves from black holes have been detected and analysed.<ref name="reynolds21" /> 

A satellite based gravitational wave instrument, [[Laser Interferometer Space Antenna|LISA]], is under construction with launch planned for approximately 2035. The mission will consist of three spacecraft flying in a triangular formation with 2.5&nbsp;million km arm lengths to detect low-frequency gravitational waves from sources such as merging supermassive black holes and compact binary systems.<ref name=eLISAconcept>{{cite web |title=eLISA: the mission concept |publisher=eLISA Consortium |url=https://www.elisascience.org/articles/elisa-mission/mission-concept |access-date=12 November 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131205051420/https://www.elisascience.org/articles/elisa-mission/mission-concept |archive-date=5 December 2013}}</ref><ref name="2017proposal">{{cite web |date=20 January 2017 |title=LISA: Laser Interferometer Space Antenna |url=https://www.elisascience.org/files/publications/LISA_L3_20170120.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171017043638/https://www.elisascience.org/files/publications/LISA_L3_20170120.pdf |archive-date=17 October 2017 |access-date=14 January 2018 |publisher=LISA Consortium}}</ref>

=== Stars orbiting Sagittarius A* ===
{{Main|Sagittarius A* cluster}}

[[File:SgrA2021 video.ogg|thumb|alt=Several stars, represented by white dots, moving around a central object (Sagittarius A*). The background switches between images taken of the area over various years.|Stars moving around Sagittarius A*, as seen in 2021]]


The Event Horizon Telescope image of Sagittarius A*, the object at the centre of the Milky Way, released in 2022, provided confirmation that it is indeed a black hole.<ref name="eht-press-release">{{Cite web |title=Astronomers Reveal First Image of the Black Hole at the Heart of Our Galaxy |url=https://eventhorizontelescope.org/blog/astronomers-reveal-first-image-black-hole-heart-our-galaxy |date=12 May 2022 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250926081853/https://eventhorizontelescope.org/blog/astronomers-reveal-first-image-black-hole-heart-our-galaxy |archive-date=26 September 2025 |access-date=2 December 2025 |website=[[Event Horizon Telescope]]}}</ref>

The [[proper motion]]s of stars near the centre of the Milky Way provide strong observational evidence that these stars are orbiting a supermassive black hole. Astronomers have tracked the motions of over 100 stars orbiting an invisible object coincident with the radio source Sagittarius A*. One of the stars—called [[S2 (star)|S2]]—completed a full orbit.  By fitting the motions of stars to [[Keplerian orbit]]s, the astronomers were able to infer that the invisible object assumed to be Sagittarius A* must have a mass of {{Solar mass|{{val|4.3e6}}}}, with a radius of less than 0.002 light-years.<ref name="Gillessen">{{cite journal |last1=Gillessen |first1=S. |last2=Eisenhauer |first2=F. |last3=Trippe |first3=S. |last4=Alexander |first4=T. |last5=Genzel |first5=R. |last6=Martins |first6=F. |last7=Ott |first7=T. |display-authors=2 |title=Monitoring Stellar Orbits Around the Massive Black Hole in the Galactic Center |journal=The Astrophysical Journal |volume=692 |issue=2 |pages=1075–1109 |date=2009 |doi=10.1088/0004-637X/692/2/1075 |arxiv=0810.4674 |bibcode=2009ApJ...692.1075G }}</ref> This upper limit radius is larger than the Schwarzschild radius for the estimated mass, so the combination does not prove Sagittarius A* is a black hole. Nevertheless, these observations strongly suggest that the central object is a supermassive black hole as there are no other plausible scenarios for confining so much invisible mass into such a small volume.<ref name="Ghez1998">{{cite journal |last1=Ghez |first1=A. M. |last2=Klein |first2=B. L. |last3=Morris |first3=M. |last4=Becklin |first4=E. E. |display-authors=2 |title=High Proper-Motion Stars in the Vicinity of Sagittarius A*: Evidence for a Supermassive Black Hole at the Center of Our Galaxy |journal=The Astrophysical Journal |volume=509 |issue=2 |pages=678–686 |date=1998 |doi=10.1086/306528 |arxiv=astro-ph/9807210 |bibcode=1998ApJ...509..678G }}</ref> Additionally, luminosity data from this object implies it must possess an event horizon, a defining feature of black holes.<ref>{{Cite journal |title=The Event Horizon of Sagittarius A* |last1=Broderick |first1=Avery |date=August 2009 |journal=The Astrophysical Journal |doi=10.1088/0004-637X/701/2/1357 |last2=Loeb |first2=Abraham |last3=Narayan |first3=Ramesh |volume=701 |issue=2 |pages=1357–1366 |arxiv=0903.1105 |bibcode=2009ApJ...701.1357B }}</ref> By tracking the motion of the centre it has been shown that the central object is motionless at the centre of the galaxy.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Reid |first1=M. J. |last2=Brunthaler |first2=A. |title=The Proper Motion of Sagittarius A*. III. The Case for a Supermassive Black Hole |journal=The Astrophysical Journal |date=2020 |volume=892 |issue=1 |page=39 |doi=10.3847/1538-4357/ab76cd |arxiv=2001.04386 |bibcode=2020ApJ...892...39R |doi-access=free }}</ref>

=== Binaries ===
{{See also|X-ray binary}}
[[File:Chandra image of Cygnus X-1.jpg|thumb|alt=Black hole Cygnus X-1 as seen by the Chandra X-Ray Observatory, a bright spot over a black background.|A [[Chandra X-Ray Observatory]] image of [[Cygnus X-1]], which was the first strong black hole candidate discovered]]

[[X-ray binaries]] are binary systems that emit significant amounts of [[X-ray radiation]]. These X-ray emissions result when a compact object accretes matter from an ordinary star.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Schatz |first1=H. |last2=Rehm |first2=K.E. |title=X-Ray Binaries |journal=Nuclear Physics A |date=2006 |volume=777 |pages=601–622 |doi=10.1016/j.nuclphysa.2005.05.200 |arxiv=astro-ph/0607624 |bibcode=2006NuPhA.777..601S }}</ref> The presence of an ordinary star in such a system provides an opportunity for studying the central object and to determine if it might be a black hole. By measuring the [[orbital period]] of the binary, the distance to the binary from Earth, and the mass of the companion star, scientists can estimate the mass of the compact object.<ref>{{Cite journal |title=Masses and Luminosities of X-Ray Binaries |journal=SIM PlanetQuest: Science with the Space Interferometry Mission |last1=Quirrenbach |first1=Andreas |url=https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/20050186757/downloads/20050186757.pdf |last2=Frink |first2=Sabine |last3=Tomsick |first3=John |publisher=[[National Aeronautics and Space Administration]] |publication-date=1 December 2004 |bibcode=2002swsi.conf...33Q |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251219042923/https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/20050186757/downloads/20050186757.pdf |archive-date=19 December 2025 |access-date=2 December 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> The [[Tolman-Oppenheimer-Volkoff limit]] (TOV limit) dictates the largest mass a nonrotating neutron star can be, and is estimated to be about two solar masses. While a rotating neutron star can be slightly more massive, if the compact object is much more massive than the TOV limit, it cannot be a neutron star and is generally expected to be a black hole.<ref name="CMS1999">{{cite journal |last1=Celotti |first1=A. |last2=Miller |first2=J. C. |last3=Sciama |first3=D. W. |title=Astrophysical evidence for the existence of black holes |journal=Classical and Quantum Gravity |date=1999 |volume=16 |issue=12A |pages=A3–A21 |doi=10.1088/0264-9381/16/12A/301 |arxiv=astro-ph/9912186 |bibcode=1999CQGra..16A...3C }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Cho |first1=Adrian |title=A Weight Limit Emerges for Neutron Stars |journal=Science |date=2018 |volume=359 |issue=6377 |pages=724–725 |doi=10.1126/science.359.6377.724 |pmid=29449468 |bibcode=2018Sci...359..724C }}</ref>

[[X-ray binaries]] can be categorised as either <em>low-mass</em> or <em>high-mass</em>; this classification is based on the mass of the companion star, not the compact object itself.<ref name="lph97" /> In a class of X-ray binaries called soft X-ray transients, the companion star is of relatively low mass, allowing for more accurate estimates of the black hole mass. These systems actively emit X-rays for only several months once every 10–50 years. During the period of low X-ray emission, called quiescence, the accretion disk is extremely faint, allowing detailed observation of the companion star.<ref name="CMS1999" /> Numerous black hole candidates have been measured by this method.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Corral-Santana |first1=J. M. |last2=Casares |first2=J. |last3=Muñoz-Darias |first3=T. |last4=Bauer |first4=F. E. |last5=Martínez-Pais |first5=I. G. |last6=Russell |first6=D. M. |display-authors=2 |title=BlackCAT: A catalogue of stellar-mass black holes in X-ray transients |journal=Astronomy & Astrophysics |date=2016 |volume=587 |pages=A61 |doi=10.1051/0004-6361/201527130  |doi-access=free|arxiv=1510.08869 |bibcode=2016A&A...587A..61C }}</ref> Black holes are also sometimes found in binaries with other compact objects, such as [[white dwarf]]s,<ref name="lph97" /> neutron stars,<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Broekgaarden |first1=Floor S. |last2=Berger |first2=Edo |title=Formation of the First Two Black Hole–Neutron Star Mergers (GW200115 and GW200105) from Isolated Binary Evolution |journal=The Astrophysical Journal Letters |date=2021 |volume=920 |issue=1 |pages=L13 |doi=10.3847/2041-8213/ac2832 |arxiv=2108.05763 |bibcode=2021ApJ...920L..13B |doi-access=free }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=Chattopadhyay |first1=Debatri |last2=Stevenson |first2=Simon |last3=Broekgaarden |first3=Floor |last4=Antonini |first4=Fabio |last5=Belczynski |first5=Krzysztof |title=Modelling the Formation of the First Two Neutron Star–Black Hole Mergers, GW200105 and GW200115: Metallicity, Chirp Masses, And Merger Remnant Spins |journal=Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society |date=2022 |volume=513 |issue=4 |pages=5780–5789 |doi=10.1093/mnras/stac1283 |doi-access=free }}</ref> and other black holes.<ref>{{Cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=Ziosi |first1=B. M. |last2=Mapelli |first2=M. |last3=Branchesi |first3=M. |last4=Tormen |first4=G. |title=Dynamics of Stellar Black Holes in Young Star Clusters with Different Metallicities – II. Black Hole-Black Hole Binaries |journal=Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society |date=2014 |volume=441 |issue=4 |pages=3703–3717 |doi=10.1093/mnras/stu824 |doi-access=free |arxiv=1404.7147 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Sources and Types of Gravitational Waves |url=https://www.ligo.caltech.edu/page/gw-sources |access-date=26 October 2025 |website=LIGO Caltech |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241001000745/https://www.ligo.caltech.edu/page/gw-sources |archive-date=1 October 2024 |url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Galactic nuclei ===
The centre of nearly every large galaxy contains a supermassive black hole.<ref name="Cattaneo-2009" /> The close observational correlation between the mass of this hole and the velocity dispersion of the host galaxy's [[galactic bulge|bulge]], known as the [[M–sigma relation]], strongly suggests a connection between the formation of the black hole and that of the galaxy itself.<ref name="King">{{cite journal |last=King |first=A. |title=Black Holes, Galaxy Formation, And the MBH-σ Relation |journal=The Astrophysical Journal Letters |volume=596 |issue=1 |pages=27–29 |date=2003 |doi=10.1086/379143 |arxiv=astro-ph/0308342|bibcode=2003ApJ...596L..27K }}</ref><ref name="msigma2000">{{cite journal |title=A Fundamental Relation Between Supermassive Black Holes and Their Host Galaxies |last1=Ferrarese |first1=L. |last2=Merritt |first2=D. |author2-link=David Merritt |journal=The Astrophysical Journal Letters |volume=539 |issue=1 |pages=9–12 |date=2000 |bibcode=2000ApJ...539L...9F |doi=10.1086/312838 |arxiv=astro-ph/0006053 }}</ref> In some galaxies, the black hole forms a powerful source of radiation called an [[active galactic nucleus]].<ref name="ff05">{{Cite journal |last1=Ferrarese |first1=Laura |last2=Ford |first2=Holland |title=Supermassive Black Holes in Galactic Nuclei: Past, Present and Future Research |journal=Space Science Reviews |date=2005 |volume=116 |issue=3–4 |pages=523–624 |doi=10.1007/s11214-005-3947-6 |arxiv=astro-ph/0411247 |bibcode=2005SSRv..116..523F }}</ref><ref name="peterson14">{{Cite journal |last1=Peterson |first1=Bradley M. |title=Measuring the Masses of Supermassive Black Holes |journal=Space Science Reviews |date=2014 |volume=183 |issue=1–4 |pages=253–275 |doi=10.1007/s11214-013-9987-4 |bibcode=2014SSRv..183..253P }}</ref>

==== Active galactic nucleus ====
{{See also|Active galactic nucleus}}
[[File:X-RayFlare-BlackHole-MilkyWay-20140105.jpg|thumb|alt=The center of the Milky Way, with an inset X-ray image of Sagittarius A*|Detection of an unusually bright [[X-ray]] flare from Sagittarius A*, a black hole in the centre of the Milky Way galaxy in September 2013<ref name="NASA-20150105">{{cite web |last1=Chou |first1=Felicia |last2=Anderson |first2=Janet |last3=Watzke |first3=Megan |date=5 January 2015 |title=RELEASE 15-001—NASA's Chandra Detects Record-Breaking Outburst from Milky Way's Black Hole |url=http://www.nasa.gov/press/2015/january/nasa-s-chandra-detects-record-breaking-outburst-from-milky-way-s-black-hole/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150106100532/http://www.nasa.gov/press/2015/january/nasa-s-chandra-detects-record-breaking-outburst-from-milky-way-s-black-hole/ |archive-date=6 January 2015 |access-date=6 January 2015 |website=[[NASA]]}}</ref>]]

Astronomers use the term ''active galaxy'' to describe galaxies with unusual characteristics, such as unusual [[spectral line]] emission and very strong radio emission. Theoretical and observational studies have shown that the high levels of activity in the centres of these galaxies, regions called active galactic nuclei (AGN), may be explained by accretion onto supermassive black holes. These AGN consist of a central black hole that may be millions or billions of times more massive than the [[Sun]], a disk of [[interstellar gas]] and dust called an accretion disk, and two [[relativistic jet|jets]] perpendicular to the accretion disk.<ref>{{multiref2|{{cite book |first=J. H. |last=Krolik |date=1999 |title=Active Galactic Nuclei |publisher=Princeton University Press |isbn=978-0-691-01151-6 |at=Ch. 1.2 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=oRK8otMiWIgC&q=Active+Galactic+Nuclei |access-date=16 October 2020 |archive-date=14 August 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210814220336/https://books.google.com/books?id=oRK8otMiWIgC&q=Active+Galactic+Nuclei |url-status=live }}|{{cite book |first1=L. S. |last1=Sparke |author-link=Linda Sparke |first2=J. S. |last2=Gallagher |date=2000 |title=Galaxies in the Universe: An Introduction |publisher=Cambridge University Press |at=Ch. 9.1 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=N8Hngab5liQC&q=Galaxies+in+the+Universe:+An+Introduction |isbn=978-0-521-59740-1 |access-date=16 October 2020 |archive-date=22 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220322141933/https://books.google.com/books?id=N8Hngab5liQC&q=Galaxies+in+the+Universe%3A+An+Introduction |url-status=live }}|{{Cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=Marconi |first1=A. |last2=Risaliti |first2=G. |last3=Gilli |first3=R. |last4=Hunt |first4=L. K. |last5=Maiolino |first5=R. |last6=Salvati |first6=M. |title=Local Supermassive Black Holes, Relics of Active Galactic Nuclei and the X-Ray Background |journal=Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society |date=2004 |volume=351 |issue=1 |pages=169–185 |doi=10.1111/j.1365-2966.2004.07765.x |arxiv=astro-ph/0311619 |bibcode=2004MNRAS.351..169M |doi-access=free }}}}</ref>
The black holes in quiescent galaxies accrete matter more slowly or radiate less efficiently.<ref name="ff05"/><ref name="peterson14"/>

Although supermassive black holes are expected to be found in most AGN, only some galaxies' nuclei have been more carefully studied in attempts to both identify and measure the actual masses of the central supermassive black hole candidates. Some of the most notable galaxies with supermassive black hole candidates include the [[Andromeda Galaxy]], [[Messier 32]], [[Messier 87]], the [[Sombrero Galaxy]], and the Milky Way itself.<ref name="kormendyrichstone1995">{{cite journal |first1=J. |last1=Kormendy |first2=D. |last2=Richstone |title=Inward Bound—The Search For Supermassive Black Holes In Galactic Nuclei |journal=Annual Review of Astronomy and Astrophysics |date=1995 |volume=33 |issue=1 |pages=581–624 |bibcode=1995ARA&A..33..581K |doi=10.1146/annurev.aa.33.090195.003053}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Melia |first1=Fulvio |last2=Falcke |first2=Heino |title=The Supermassive Black Hole at the Galactic Center |journal=Annual Review of Astronomy and Astrophysics |date=2001 |volume=39 |pages=309–352 |doi=10.1146/annurev.astro.39.1.309 |arxiv=astro-ph/0106162 |bibcode=2001ARA&A..39..309M }}</ref>

=== Microlensing ===
[[File:Gravitational microlensing by black hole - cropped.jpg|thumb|alt=A diagram of gravitational microlensing: A foreground black hole warps light from a background star, creating two images of the star. The light then travels to Earth-based telescopes, where the two images cannot be resolved and appear solely as a single brightened star.|The intense gravitational field of a foreground black hole acts like a powerful lens, distorting and brightening the image of a background star.]]
Black holes can be detected by [[gravitational lensing]]: the deflection of light rays by the deformation of spacetime around a massive object.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Wambsganss |first1=Joachim |title=Gravitational Lensing in Astronomy |journal=Living Reviews in Relativity |date=1998 |volume=1 |issue=1 |article-number=12 |doi=10.12942/lrr-1998-12 |pmid=28937183 |pmc=5567250 |arxiv=astro-ph/9812021 |bibcode=1998LRR.....1...12W |doi-access=free }}</ref> A distant star behind a source of gravity may produce multiple images of that star but if the images cannot be [[angular resolution|resolved]], the phenomenon is called [[microlensing]].<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Wambsganss |first1=J. |title=Gravitational Lensing: Strong, Weak and Micro |chapter=Gravitational Microlensing |series=Saas-Fee Advanced Courses |date=2006 |volume=33 |pages=453–540 |doi=10.1007/978-3-540-30310-7_4 |arxiv=astro-ph/0604278 |isbn=978-3-540-30309-1 }}</ref> In microlensing, astronomers see the star magnified by an amount which changes as the source star, lens, and observer move. Astronomers must monitor the star image over a few years and match its light curve to models of the gravitational effect.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Abrams |first1=Natasha S. |last2=Takada |first2=Masahiro |date=1 December 2020 |title=Hunting Gravitational Wave Black Holes with Microlensing |journal=The Astrophysical Journal |volume=905 |issue=2 |pages=121 |doi=10.3847/1538-4357/abc6aa |doi-access=free |arxiv=2006.05578 |bibcode=2020ApJ...905..121A |issn=0004-637X}}</ref> As a tool for astrophysics, microlensing is uniquely sensitive to dark objects like isolated black holes not paired in a binary object. However comparison of the predicted light curve to observations yields multiple indistinguishable solutions, requiring expensive follow-up measurements to select and confirm candidate black holes. Over 10,000 microlensing events yielded 23 black hole candidates but only one object has been confirmed as an isolated black hole using additional measurements from the [[Hubble Space Telescope]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Kaczmarek |first1=Zofia |last2=McGill |first2=Peter |last3=Perkins |first3=Scott E. |last4=Dawson |first4=William A. |last5=Huston |first5=Macy |last6=Ho |first6=Ming-Feng |last7=Abrams |first7=Natasha S. |last8=Lu |first8=Jessica R. |date=7 March 2025 |title=On Finding Black Holes in Photometric Microlensing Surveys |journal=The Astrophysical Journal |volume=981 |issue=2 |pages=183 |doi=10.3847/1538-4357/adb1d7 |doi-access=free |arxiv=2410.14098 |bibcode=2025ApJ...981..183K |issn=0004-637X}}</ref>

== Areas of investigation ==
=== Information loss paradox ===
{{Main|Black hole information paradox}}

{{unsolved|physics|Is physical information lost in black holes?}}

According to the no-hair theorem, a black hole is defined by only three parameters: its mass, charge, and angular momentum. This seems to mean that all other information about the matter that went into forming the black hole is lost, as there is no way to determine anything about the black hole from outside other than those three parameters. When black holes were thought to persist forever, this information loss was not problematic, as the information can be thought of as existing inside the black hole.<ref name="PlayDice000">{{cite web |title=Does God Play Dice? |first=S. W. |last=Hawking |url=http://www.hawking.org.uk/does-god-play-dice.html |access-date=14 March 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120111012413/http://www.hawking.org.uk/does-god-play-dice.html |archive-date=11 January 2012 }}{{self-published inline|date=April 2026}}</ref> However, black holes slowly evaporate by emitting Hawking radiation. This radiation does not appear to carry any additional information about the matter that formed the black hole, meaning that this information is seemingly gone forever. This is called the black hole information paradox.<ref name="math_ucr_edu">{{cite web |url=http://math.ucr.edu/home/baez/physics/Relativity/BlackHoles/info_loss.html |title=The Black Hole Information Loss Problem |website=Usenet Physics FAQ |last=Anderson |first=Warren G. |date=1996 |access-date=24 March 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090122223839/http://math.ucr.edu/home/baez/physics/Relativity/BlackHoles/info_loss.html |archive-date=22 January 2009}}</ref><ref name="Preskill1994">{{cite conference |last=Preskill |first=J. |author-link1=John Preskill |url=http://www.theory.caltech.edu/~preskill/talks/blackholes.pdf |title=Black Holes and Information: A Crisis in Quantum Physics |date=21 October 1994 |conference=Caltech Theory Seminar |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080518054438/http://www.theory.caltech.edu/~preskill/talks/blackholes.pdf |archive-date=18 May 2008 |access-date=17 May 2009 }}</ref> Theoretical studies analysing the paradox have led to both further paradoxes and new ideas about the intersection of quantum mechanics and general relativity. While there is no consensus on the resolution of the paradox, work on the problem is expected to be important for a theory of [[quantum gravity]].<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Raju |first1=Suvrat |title=Lessons from the information paradox |journal=Physics Reports |date=2022 |volume=943 |pages=1–80 |doi=10.1016/j.physrep.2021.10.001 |arxiv=2012.05770 |bibcode=2022PhR...943....1R }}</ref>{{rp|126}}

=== Supermassive black holes in the early universe ===
[[File:High-Redshift Quasar and Companion Galaxy (Illustration) (2020-51-4755-Image).png|thumb|alt=Two spiral galaxies, one of which has a very bright center |Two galaxies from the first billion years after the Big Bang. The galaxy on the left hosts a luminous quasar at its centre.]]
Observations of faraway galaxies have found that ultraluminous quasars, powered by supermassive black holes, existed in the early universe as far back as redshift <math>z\ge7,</math> less than a billion years after the Big Bang.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Volonteri |first1=Marta |last2=Habouzit |first2=Mélanie |last3=Colpi |first3=Monica |date=20 September 2021 |title=The origins of massive black holes |url=https://www.nature.com/articles/s42254-021-00364-9 |journal=Nature Reviews Physics |language=en |volume=3 |issue=11 |pages=732–743 |doi=10.1038/s42254-021-00364-9 |arxiv=2110.10175 |bibcode=2021NatRP...3..732V |issn=2522-5820 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250225132206/https://www.nature.com/articles/s42254-021-00364-9 |archive-date=25 February 2025 |access-date=23 April 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> These black holes have been assumed to be the products of the gravitational collapse of large [[population III star]]s.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Trenti |first1=M. |last2=Stiavelli |first2=M. |title=Distribution of the Very First Population III Stars and Their Relation to Bright ''z'' ≈ 6 Quasars |journal=The Astrophysical Journal |date=2007 |volume=667 |issue=1 |pages=38–48 |doi=10.1086/520502 |arxiv=0705.3843 |bibcode=2007ApJ...667...38T }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Singh |first1=Jasbir |last2=Monaco |first2=Pierluigi |last3=Tan |first3=Jonathan C. |title=The Formation of Supermassive Black Holes from Population III.1 Seeds. II. Evolution to the Local Universe |journal=Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society |date=2023 |volume=525 |pages=969–982 |doi=10.1093/mnras/stad2346 |doi-access=free }}</ref> However, these stellar remnants were not massive enough to produce the quasars observed at early times without accreting beyond the Eddington limit, the theoretical maximum rate of black hole accretion.<ref name="sb19">{{Cite journal |last1=Smith |first1=Aaron |last2=Bromm |first2=Volker |title=Supermassive Black Holes in the Early Universe |journal=Contemporary Physics |date=2019 |volume=60 |issue=2 |pages=111–126 |doi=10.1080/00107514.2019.1615715 |arxiv=1904.12890 |bibcode=2019ConPh..60..111S }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=Jeon |first1=Myoungwon |last2=Pawlik |first2=Andreas H. |last3=Bromm |first3=Volker |last4=Milosavljević |first4=Miloš |title=Radiative Feedback from High-Mass X-Ray Binaries on the Formation of the First Galaxies and Early Reionization |journal=Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society |date=2014 |volume=440 |issue=4 |pages=3778–3796 |doi=10.1093/mnras/stu444 |doi-access=free |arxiv=1310.7944 }}</ref>

Physicists have suggested a variety of different mechanisms by which these supermassive black holes may have formed. It has been proposed that smaller black holes may have also undergone mergers to produce the observed supermassive black holes.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Miralda-Escudé |first1=Jaiyul Yoo Jordi |last2=Miralda-Escudé |first2=Jordi |title=Formation of the Black Holes in the Highest Redshift Quasars |journal=The Astrophysical Journal |date=2004 |volume=614 |issue=1 |pages=L25–L28 |doi=10.1086/425416 |arxiv=astro-ph/0406217 |bibcode=2004ApJ...614L..25Y }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Trakhtenbrot |first1=Benny |title=What Do Observations Tell Us About the Highest-Redshift Supermassive Black Holes? |journal=Proceedings of the International Astronomical Union |date=2019 |volume=15 |pages=261–275 |doi=10.1017/S1743921320003087 |arxiv=2002.00972 }}</ref> It is also possible that they were seeded by [[direct-collapse black hole]]s, in which a large cloud of hot gas avoids fragmentation that would lead to multiple stars, due to low angular momentum or heating from a nearby galaxy. Given the right circumstances, a single supermassive star forms and collapses directly into a black hole without undergoing typical [[stellar evolution]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Mayer |first1=Lucio |last2=Bonoli |first2=Silvia |title=The Route to Massive Black Hole Formation via Merger-Driven Direct Collapse: A Review |journal=Reports on Progress in Physics |date=2019 |volume=82 |issue=1 |page=016901 |doi=10.1088/1361-6633/aad6a5 |pmid=30057369 |arxiv=1803.06391 |bibcode=2019RPPh...82a6901M }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=Agarwal |first1=Bhaskar |last2=Dalla Vecchia |first2=Claudio |last3=Johnson |first3=Jarrett L. |last4=Khochfar |first4=Sadegh |last5=Paardekooper |first5=Jan-Pieter |title=The First Billion Years Project: Birthplaces of Direct Collapse Black Holes |journal=Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society |date=2014 |volume=443 |pages=648–657 |doi=10.1093/mnras/stu1112 |doi-access=free |arxiv=1403.5267 }}</ref> Additionally, these supermassive black holes in the early universe may be high-mass primordial black holes, which could have accreted further matter in the centres of galaxies.<ref>{{Cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=Shinohara |first1=Takumi |last2=He |first2=Wanqiu |last3=Matsuoka |first3=Yoshiki |last4=Nagao |first4=Tohru |last5=Suyama |first5=Teruaki |last6=Takahashi |first6=Tomo |title=Supermassive Primordial Black Holes: A View from Clustering of Quasars at <math>z\sim6</math> |journal=Physical Review D |date=2023 |volume=108 |issue=6 |article-number=063510 |doi=10.1103/PhysRevD.108.063510}}</ref> Finally, certain mechanisms allow black holes to grow faster than the theoretical Eddington limit, such as dense gas in the accretion disk limiting outward radiation pressure that prevents the black hole from accreting.<ref name="sb19" /><ref>{{Cite book |last1=Mayer |first1=Lucio |title=Formation of the First Black Holes |chapter=Super-Eddington accretion; flow regimes and conditions in high-z galaxies |date=2019 |pages=195–222 |doi=10.1142/9789813227958_0011 |arxiv=1807.06243 |isbn=978-981-322-794-1 }}</ref> However, the formation of bipolar jets prevents super-Eddington rates.<ref name="Regan" />

===Alternatives to black holes===
{{See also|Exotic star}}
While there is a strong case for supermassive black holes,<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Maoz |first1=Eyal |title=Dynamical Constraints on Alternatives to Supermassive Black Holes in Galactic Nuclei |journal=The Astrophysical Journal |date=1998 |volume=494 |issue=2 |pages=L181–L184 |doi=10.1086/311194 |arxiv=astro-ph/9710309 |bibcode=1998ApJ...494L.181M }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Miller |first1=M. Coleman |title=Constraints on Alternatives to Supermassive Black Holes |journal=Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society: Letters |date=2006 |volume=367 |issue=1 |pages=L32–L36 |doi=10.1111/j.1745-3933.2006.00135.x |arxiv=astro-ph/0512194 |bibcode=2006MNRAS.367L..32M |doi-access=free }}</ref> the dividing line between lighter black holes and neutron stars relies on theories of extremely dense matter. Direct observational tests are not available: objects observed to have mass higher than the predictions for neutron stars are assumed to be black holes. Recent evidence from gravitational wave events suggests modifications of these theories may be needed.<ref name="Vink-2021"/> New exotic [[state of matter|phases of matter]] could allow other kinds of massive objects.<ref name="CMS1999" /> [[Quark star]]s would be made up of [[quark matter]] and supported by quark degeneracy pressure, a form of degeneracy pressure even stronger than neutron degeneracy pressure. This would halt gravitational collapse at a higher mass than for a neutron star.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Kovacs |first1=Z. |last2=Cheng |first2=K. S. |last3=Harko |first3=T. |title=Can Stellar Mass Black Holes Be Quark Stars? |journal=[[Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society]] | date=2009 |volume=400 |issue=3 |pages=1632–1642 |doi=10.1111/j.1365-2966.2009.15571.x |doi-access=free |arxiv=0908.2672 |bibcode=2009MNRAS.400.1632K }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Sotani |first1=Hajime |last2=Kohri |first2=Kazunori |last3=Harada |first3=Tomohiro |title=Restricting Quark Matter Models by Gravitational Wave Observation |journal=Physical Review D |date=2004 |volume=69 |issue=8 |article-number=084008 |doi=10.1103/PhysRevD.69.084008 |arxiv=gr-qc/0310079 |bibcode=2004PhRvD..69h4008S }}</ref> Even stronger stars called [[electroweak star]]s would convert quarks in their cores into [[lepton]]s, providing additional pressure to stop the star from collapsing.<ref name="bonkowsky25">{{Cite web |title=Between Neutron Stars and Black Holes |url=https://www.thecolumbiasciencereview.com/online-articles/between-neutron-stars-and-black-holes |last=Bonkowsky |first=Charles |date=5 January 2025 |access-date=6 December 2025 |website=Columbia Science Review |archive-date=14 December 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251214040325/https://www.thecolumbiasciencereview.com/online-articles/between-neutron-stars-and-black-holes |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=Dai |first1=De-Chang |last2=Lue |first2=Arthur |last3=Starkman |first3=Glenn |last4=Stojkovic |first4=Dejan |title=Electroweak Stars: How Nature May Capitalize on the Standard Model's Ultimate Fuel |journal=Journal of Cosmology and Astroparticle Physics |date=2010 |issue=12 |page=004 |doi=10.1088/1475-7516/2010/12/004 |arxiv=0912.0520 |bibcode=2010JCAP...12..004D }}</ref> If, as some extensions of the [[Standard Model]] posit, [[quark]]s and leptons are made up of the even-smaller fundamental particles called [[preon]]s, a very compact star could be supported by preon degeneracy pressure.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Hansson |first1=J. |last2=Sandin |first2=F. |title=Preon Stars: A New Class of Cosmic Compact Objects |journal=Physics Letters B |volume=616 |issue=1–2 |pages=1–7 |date=2005 |doi=10.1016/j.physletb.2005.04.034 |arxiv=astro-ph/0410417 |bibcode=2005PhLB..616....1H }}</ref> While none of these hypothetical models can explain all of the observations of stellar black hole candidates, a [[Q star]] is the only alternative which could significantly exceed the mass limit for neutron stars and thus provide an alternative for supermassive black holes.<ref name="CMS1999" />{{rp|12}}

A few theoretical objects have been conjectured to match observations of astronomical black hole candidates identically or near-identically, but which function via a different mechanism.<ref name="Murk2023">{{cite journal |last=Murk |first=Sebastian |title=Nomen Non Est Omen: Why It Is Too Soon to Identify Ultra-Compact Objects as Black Holes |journal=International Journal of Modern Physics D |year=2023 |volume=32 |issue=14 |pages=2342012–2342235 |article-number=2342012 |doi=10.1142/S0218271823420129 |arxiv=2210.03750 |bibcode=2023IJMPD..3242012M }}</ref> A [[dark energy star]] would convert infalling matter into [[vacuum energy]]; this vacuum energy would be much larger than the vacuum energy of outside space, exerting outwards pressure and preventing a singularity from forming.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Bagheri Tudeshki |first1=A. |last2=Bordbar |first2=G.H. |last3=Eslam Panah |first3=B. |title=Dark Energy Star in Gravity's Rainbow |journal=Physics Letters B |date=2022 |volume=835 |article-number=137523 |doi=10.1016/j.physletb.2022.137523 |arxiv=2208.07063 |bibcode=2022PhLB..83537523B }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Ball |first1=Philip |title=Black Holes 'Do Not Exist' |journal=Nature |date=31 March 2005 |doi=10.1038/news050328-8}}</ref> A [[black star (semiclassical gravity)|black star]] would be gravitationally collapsing slowly enough that quantum effects would keep it just on the cusp of fully collapsing into a black hole.<ref>{{Cite journal |display-authors=2 |last1=Barceló |first1=Carlos |last2=Liberati |first2=Stefano |last3=Sonego |first3=Sebastiano |last4=Visser |first4=Matt |title=Fate of Gravitational Collapse in Semiclassical Gravity |journal=Physical Review D |date=2008 |volume=77 |issue=4 |article-number=044032 |doi=10.1103/PhysRevD.77.044032 |arxiv=0712.1130 |bibcode=2008PhRvD..77d4032B }}</ref> A [[gravastar]] would consist of a very thin shell and a dark-energy interior providing outward pressure to stop the collapse into a black hole or formation of a singularity; It could even have another gravastar inside, called a 'nestar'.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Jampolski |first1=Daniel |last2=Rezzolla |first2=Luciano |title=Nested Solutions of Gravitational Condensate Stars |journal=Classical and Quantum Gravity |date=2024 |volume=41 |issue=6 |doi=10.1088/1361-6382/ad2317 |arxiv=2310.13946 |bibcode=2024CQGra..41f5014J }}</ref>

== In fiction ==
{{Further|Black holes in fiction}}
[[File:Interstellar black hole (no lens flare).jpg|thumb|alt=A black hole over a black background surrounded by a light orange accretion disk. The disk wraps around the top and bottom of the black hole, and across the front like a crossbar. A white, mostly-circular photon sphere is slightly inset inside the black hole's shadow.|The black hole and accretion disk used in the movie ''[[Interstellar (film)|Interstellar]]'', without lens flare. The visual effects team used relativity to visualize gravitational lensing around the black hole.<ref name="jtft15" />]]

The concept of black holes has inspired artists as well as scientists.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ouellette |first=Jennifer |date=26 December 2025 |title=Embark on a visual voyage of art inspired by black holes |url=https://arstechnica.com/science/2025/12/embark-on-a-visual-voyage-of-art-inspired-by-black-holes/ |access-date=6 April 2026 |website=Ars Technica |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260310104938/https://arstechnica.com/science/2025/12/embark-on-a-visual-voyage-of-art-inspired-by-black-holes/ |archive-date=10 March 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> In her book ''Conjuring the Void: the Art of Black Holes,'' [[Lynn Gamwell]] used black holes as an example to explore how art and science interact, considering the application of art to create scientific visualizations and the impact of scientific ideas on art concepts like darkness.<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Gamwell |first1=Lynn |title=Conjuring the void: the art of black holes |last2=Tyson |first2=Neil deGrasse |date=2025 |publisher=The MIT Press |isbn=978-0-262-04996-2 |location=Cambridge}}</ref> Some science fiction films have incorporated relativity into their black hole visualizations, leading to results similar to images derived from the [[Event Horizon Telescope]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=How 'High Life' Created a Black Hole That Looks Just Like the Historic Photo |url=https://www.inverse.com/article/55087-high-life-claire-denis-aurelien-barrau-got-black-holes-right |last=Tayag |first=Yasmin |date=20 April 2019 |access-date=31 March 2026 |website=Inverse |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260608015335/https://www.inverse.com/article/55087-high-life-claire-denis-aurelien-barrau-got-black-holes-right |archive-date=8 June 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="jtft15" /> Fictional treatments of black holes are also used as a mechanism for teaching science.<ref name="kyle19">{{Cite magazine |date=19 June 2019 |title=Understanding Black Holes Through Science Fiction |magazine=Sci Phi Journal |last=Johnson |first=David Kyle |url=https://www.sciphijournal.org/index.php/2019/06/19/understanding-black-holes-through-science-fiction/ |access-date=20 December 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200920043430/https://www.sciphijournal.org/index.php/2019/06/19/understanding-black-holes-through-science-fiction/ |archive-date=20 September 2020 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="FraknoiBlackHoles">{{Cite web |last=Fraknoi |first=Andrew |author-link=Andrew Fraknoi |date=January 2024 |title=Science Fiction Stories with Good Astronomy & Physics: A Topical Index |url=https://astrosociety.org/file_download/inline/7b5edc23-7a89-46c1-a6b3-33a30ed4c876 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240210011957/https://astrosociety.org/file_download/inline/7b5edc23-7a89-46c1-a6b3-33a30ed4c876 |archive-date=10 February 2024 |archive-format=PDF |access-date=21 June 2024 |website=[[Astronomical Society of the Pacific]] | pages=3–4 |format=PDF |edition=7.3}}</ref>

Black holes have been portrayed in science fiction in a variety of ways. Even before the advent of the term itself, objects with characteristics of black holes appeared in stories.<ref name="WestfahlBlackHoles">{{Cite book |last=Westfahl |first=Gary |title=[[Science Fiction Literature Through History: An Encyclopedia]] | date=2021 |publisher=ABC-CLIO |isbn=978-1-4408-6617-3 |pages=159–162 |language=en |chapter=Black Holes |author-link=Gary Westfahl |chapter-url=https://books.google.com/books?id=WETPEAAAQBAJ&pg=PA159}}</ref> In modern day, authors and screenwriters have exploited the relativistic effects of black holes, particularly gravitational time dilation. Black holes have also been appropriated as wormholes or other methods of [[faster-than-light travel]].<ref name="StablefordBlackHole">{{Cite book |last=Stableford |first=Brian |title=[[Science Fact and Science Fiction: An Encyclopedia]] | date=2006 |publisher=Taylor & Francis |isbn=978-0-415-97460-8 |pages=65–67 |language=en |chapter=Black Hole |author-link=Brian Stableford |chapter-url=https://books.google.com/books?id=uefwmdROKTAC&pg=PA65}}</ref>
